WEBVTT


00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.500
Downloaded @ subs4free.club

﻿1

00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:48.040
14, 15...


00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:50.953
16, prime...


00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:53.315
18, prime...


00:00:53.960 --> 00:00:55.393
20...


00:00:55.960 --> 00:00:57.473
21...


00:00:57.640 --> 00:00:58.993
22, prime...


00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:00.496
West Virginia
Collegiate Institute


00:01:00.520 --> 00:01:03.159
is the best school for
Negros in the state.


00:01:03.320 --> 00:01:05.595
It's the only school
past the eighth grade


00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:06.680
anywhere near here.


00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:08.793
Isosceles.


00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:10.873
Scalene.


00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:12.632
Equilateral.


00:01:13.480 --> 00:01:14.708
Rhombus.


00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:16.677
Trapezoid.


00:01:16.840 --> 00:01:18.831
Katherine's in the sixth grade.


00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:20.056
They want to
take her early.


00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:21.718
Tetrahedron.


00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:24.558
Dodecahedron.


00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:26.416
They're offering
a full scholarship.


00:01:26.440 --> 00:01:27.919
All you have to do
is get there.


00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:30.829
Ms. Coleman...


00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:34.711
why don't you solve
the equation on the board?


00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:43.192
We took up a collection


00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:44.679
amongst the teachers and such.


00:01:44.840 --> 00:01:45.840
It's not a lot...


00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:48.115
but it's enough
to help get you settled in.


00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:51.509
That's more than kind,
Ms. Sumner.


00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:04.776
If the product of two terms


00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:06.870
is zero then common sense says


00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:10.117
at least one of the two terms
has to be zero to start with.


00:02:10.280 --> 00:02:13.192
So if you move all the
terms over to one side,


00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:15.391
you can put the quadratics
into a form


00:02:15.480 --> 00:02:16.708
that can be factored...


00:02:16.880 --> 00:02:18.472
allowing that side
of the equation


00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:19.560
to equal zero.


00:02:20.440 --> 00:02:21.856
Once you've done that,
it's pretty straightforward


00:02:21.880 --> 00:02:22.880
from there.


00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:27.920
In all my years
of teaching...


00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:31.393
I've never seen a mind like
the one your daughter has.


00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:33.400
Katherine!


00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:35.232
Hurry on now, love.


00:02:35.800 --> 00:02:37.279
You have to go.


00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:39.752
Let Mama tuck you in.


00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:48.594
You have to see
what she becomes.


00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:16.318
Okay,
try to turn it over now.


00:03:17.800 --> 00:03:18.949
Katherine?


00:03:19.520 --> 00:03:20.794
Mary!


00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:22.280
Somebody!


00:03:22.400 --> 00:03:23.400
Katherine!


00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:25.954
Quit staring off into space
and turn the damn car over.


00:03:26.120 --> 00:03:27.269
I got it.


00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:30.149
I'm not deaf.


00:03:30.320 --> 00:03:31.320
I wonder sometimes.


00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:33.880
Here it goes.


00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:38.790
Yeah,
it's the starter.


00:03:38.960 --> 00:03:41.793
That is definitely the starter.


00:03:41.960 --> 00:03:43.791
This starter is starting
to make us late.


00:03:43.960 --> 00:03:45.400
We are all gonna
end up unemployed...


00:03:45.480 --> 00:03:47.869
riding around in this pile
of junk to work every day.


00:03:48.080 --> 00:03:49.798
You're welcome
to walk the 16 miles.


00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:51.991
Or sit in the back of the bus.


00:03:53.200 --> 00:03:54.792
I won't do neither.


00:03:55.480 --> 00:03:56.674
I'll hitchhike.


00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:02.353
Girls.


00:04:08.240 --> 00:04:09.593
No crime in a
broken-down car.


00:04:10.280 --> 00:04:11.998
No crime being Negro neither.


00:04:12.160 --> 00:04:13.309
Button it up, Mary.


00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:16.389
Nobody wants to go to
jail behind your mouth.


00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:18.080
I'll do my best, sugar.


00:04:30.840 --> 00:04:33.229
Not a great place for three of
y'all be having car trouble.


00:04:33.880 --> 00:04:36.440
We didn't pick the place,
Officer. It picked us.


00:04:36.600 --> 00:04:38.113
You being disrespectful?


00:04:38.280 --> 00:04:39.679
No, sir.


00:04:40.200 --> 00:04:41.428
You have
identification on you?


00:04:41.600 --> 00:04:43.079
Yes, sir.
Yes, sir.


00:04:45.080 --> 00:04:47.116
We're just on our way
to work...


00:04:47.280 --> 00:04:48.349
at Langley.


00:04:49.400 --> 00:04:50.879
NASA, sir.


00:04:51.040 --> 00:04:52.553
We do a great deal
of the calculating.


00:04:52.720 --> 00:04:54.392
Getting our rockets
into space.


00:04:54.600 --> 00:04:56.079
All three of you?
Yes, sir.


00:04:56.240 --> 00:04:57.240
Yes, Officer.


00:04:58.720 --> 00:04:59.720
NASA.


00:04:59.880 --> 00:05:01.074
Now that's something.


00:05:02.040 --> 00:05:04.474
I had no idea
they hired...


00:05:04.640 --> 00:05:08.235
There are quite a few women
working in the space program.


00:05:16.920 --> 00:05:18.911
Damn Russians are
watching us right now.


00:05:19.640 --> 00:05:20.914
Sputniks.


00:05:22.280 --> 00:05:24.077
You girls ever
meet those astronauts?


00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:25.639
Mercury Seven?


00:05:25.840 --> 00:05:27.239
Absolutely.


00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:31.798
Uh, yes, sir. We work with
those gentlemen all the time.


00:05:31.920 --> 00:05:34.150
Those boys are the best
we got. I'm sure of that.


00:05:34.320 --> 00:05:35.355
Yeah.
Yeah.


00:05:35.480 --> 00:05:37.216
We gotta get a man up there
before the commies do.


00:05:37.240 --> 00:05:38.240
Yes.
Absolutely.


00:05:38.320 --> 00:05:39.776
Whole damn country's
counting on them.


00:05:39.800 --> 00:05:40.915
That's for certain.


00:05:41.080 --> 00:05:42.638
Hard being of service
broken down


00:05:42.760 --> 00:05:44.478
on the side
of the road, though.


00:05:44.760 --> 00:05:46.352
Right, right.


00:05:46.520 --> 00:05:48.192
Well, do y'all need a tow
or something?


00:05:48.360 --> 00:05:49.960
No, thank you, Officer.
I think I got it.


00:05:50.120 --> 00:05:51.189
Just give me my...


00:05:51.360 --> 00:05:54.636
Just need to bypass
the starter.


00:05:57.040 --> 00:05:58.996
She's good at this stuff.


00:05:59.600 --> 00:06:01.750
Whoo! Attagirl.


00:06:02.880 --> 00:06:03.880
We're all set.


00:06:04.040 --> 00:06:06.429
Well, hell, least I can do
is give y'all an escort.


00:06:06.600 --> 00:06:08.158
I imagine you're
running late to work.


00:06:08.320 --> 00:06:09.992
No, sir. We wouldn't
wanna bother you.


00:06:10.160 --> 00:06:11.440
That would be
wonderful, Officer.


00:06:12.720 --> 00:06:13.994
Thank you so much, sir.


00:06:14.080 --> 00:06:15.080
Follow me.


00:06:15.160 --> 00:06:16.160
I'm driving.


00:06:16.240 --> 00:06:18.016
Hurry up, Dorothy,
before he changes his mind.


00:06:18.040 --> 00:06:20.190
- We're coming.
- Hold your horses.


00:06:26.880 --> 00:06:28.096
Yes!
Whoa, slow down, Mary!


00:06:28.120 --> 00:06:29.599
Mary, slow down!
You're too close!


00:06:29.680 --> 00:06:30.816
Be quiet.
He said to follow him.


00:06:30.840 --> 00:06:32.034
He didn't mean
up his behind!


00:06:35.320 --> 00:06:37.311
Dear Lord, I don't even
know where to begin!


00:06:37.480 --> 00:06:38.833
Oh, I'll tell you
where to begin.


00:06:39.040 --> 00:06:41.634
Three Negro women are chasing
a white police officer


00:06:41.800 --> 00:06:43.552
down the highway
in Hampton, Virginia...


00:06:43.720 --> 00:06:45.551
1961.


00:06:45.720 --> 00:06:49.554
Ladies, that there
is a God-ordained miracle.


00:06:50.760 --> 00:06:53.672
And, tomorrow,
I'm riding the bus.


00:06:58.040 --> 00:06:59.758
I'm moving over


00:07:04.200 --> 00:07:06.509
I'm on a sidewalk


00:07:06.600 --> 00:07:08.431
I'm going over


00:07:15.400 --> 00:07:17.914
Lift off
is good. Trajectory is stable.


00:07:19.080 --> 00:07:20.195
Show me.


00:07:21.240 --> 00:07:22.514
No, exactly.


00:07:22.680 --> 00:07:24.955
40.349110 degrees North.


00:07:29.920 --> 00:07:32.354
Ascent angle 46. 56.


00:07:32.880 --> 00:07:35.519
Approaching
7,400 miles per hour.


00:07:37.120 --> 00:07:38.553
118 seconds.


00:07:41.400 --> 00:07:43.072
Boosters have disengaged.


00:07:48.200 --> 00:07:49.599
Second stage success.


00:07:52.240 --> 00:07:54.400
Korabl-Sputnik 4 is orbital.


00:07:56.280 --> 00:07:58.430
Orbital entry is established.


00:07:58.600 --> 00:08:00.192
Russian orbital entry
is established.


00:08:02.600 --> 00:08:03.794
Jim Webb.


00:08:05.080 --> 00:08:06.559
Yes, Mr. President.


00:08:07.240 --> 00:08:08.912
We certainly are, sir.


00:08:10.160 --> 00:08:11.912
Uh, it looks like
they've achieved


00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:13.353
at least one orbit, maybe two.


00:08:15.560 --> 00:08:18.154
Chernushka is ready
to go again.


00:08:18.320 --> 00:08:22.791
So is our comrade, Ivan,
a brave space traveler.


00:08:22.960 --> 00:08:25.599
We have proven life can
be sustained in space.


00:08:25.760 --> 00:08:27.830
Now we will be first
to send a human.


00:08:28.000 --> 00:08:30.992
A damn dog
and a damn mannequin.


00:08:33.440 --> 00:08:38.275
And then, a 1.6 megaton RDS-37
thermonuclear warhead...


00:08:38.920 --> 00:08:41.150
drops down in the middle
of Des Moines.


00:08:41.320 --> 00:08:42.720
Well, that's
a pretty big jump, sir.


00:08:42.800 --> 00:08:43.800
What?


00:08:43.880 --> 00:08:46.110
Just that...
Who the hell is he?


00:08:46.280 --> 00:08:48.316
Paul Stafford,
our lead engineer, Mr. Webb.


00:08:48.480 --> 00:08:49.680
I think what
he meant to say...


00:08:49.880 --> 00:08:52.240
is that speculation, at least
on our side of the equation...


00:08:52.320 --> 00:08:54.311
by definition can be...


00:08:54.480 --> 00:08:56.118
just a little dangerous.


00:08:56.280 --> 00:08:57.315
Dangerous?


00:08:57.480 --> 00:08:59.948
You know what's dangerous,
Mr. Stafford?


00:09:00.120 --> 00:09:02.156
Inaction and indecision.


00:09:02.800 --> 00:09:04.950
The Russians
have a spy satellite


00:09:05.040 --> 00:09:06.040
lapping the planet,


00:09:06.200 --> 00:09:08.270
taking pictures
of God knows what!


00:09:09.480 --> 00:09:11.789
The President is demanding
an immediate response.


00:09:11.960 --> 00:09:13.520
No more delay.
Alan Shepard, John Glenn,


00:09:13.640 --> 00:09:14.776
your uncle Bob,
it doesn't matter.


00:09:14.800 --> 00:09:15.869
Get us up there, Harrison.


00:09:16.040 --> 00:09:18.235
We can't justify
a space program


00:09:18.400 --> 00:09:20.675
that doesn't put
anything in space.


00:09:22.640 --> 00:09:24.471
How'd that feel
in there, Paul?


00:09:24.640 --> 00:09:26.631
You think you enlightened
the administrator?


00:09:27.560 --> 00:09:28.560
Hmm?


00:09:28.800 --> 00:09:30.870
No, he didn't look
enlightened to me.


00:09:31.320 --> 00:09:32.992
He look enlightened to you, Sam?


00:09:33.160 --> 00:09:34.229
Not particularly.


00:09:34.400 --> 00:09:35.469
And just so you know,


00:09:35.560 --> 00:09:37.136
he's not wrong about
what he said in there.


00:09:37.160 --> 00:09:38.559
You know that, right?


00:09:38.760 --> 00:09:40.432
Because now that
they can get up there,


00:09:40.520 --> 00:09:41.669
a bomb will follow.


00:09:42.400 --> 00:09:44.675
It's what happens
to all our good ideas.


00:09:45.160 --> 00:09:47.071
I imagine theirs too.
Where's...


00:09:47.520 --> 00:09:48.714
Where's the machine?


00:09:48.880 --> 00:09:49.880
Any day now,
Mr. Harrison.


00:09:50.360 --> 00:09:52.157
Any day now.
Yes, sir.


00:09:52.360 --> 00:09:54.669
What's it called?
The initials.


00:09:54.840 --> 00:09:56.034
The IBM.


00:09:56.240 --> 00:09:57.719
International Business Machines.


00:09:57.880 --> 00:10:00.553
Space is a business.


00:10:00.760 --> 00:10:02.557
I need a mathematician.


00:10:02.720 --> 00:10:04.000
I'll put in
another request, sir.


00:10:04.040 --> 00:10:05.280
"Another request."
Jesus Christ.


00:10:05.440 --> 00:10:07.556
We don't have a single person
in this entire building


00:10:07.720 --> 00:10:10.029
that can handle
analytic geometry?


00:10:10.200 --> 00:10:11.240
That's what
I've been told.


00:10:11.360 --> 00:10:12.496
Tell me something else, Ruth...


00:10:12.520 --> 00:10:13.953
like we're gonna find
such a person


00:10:14.120 --> 00:10:15.519
before the Russians
plant a flag


00:10:15.600 --> 00:10:16.600
on the damn moon.


00:10:26.760 --> 00:10:27.760
A real good time.


00:10:27.920 --> 00:10:29.000
All right, Gloria.


00:10:29.040 --> 00:10:30.680
You'll be with engineering,
building four.


00:10:32.760 --> 00:10:33.875
Ladies, ladies.


00:10:34.040 --> 00:10:35.576
If you're tardy,
you don't get an assignment.


00:10:35.600 --> 00:10:36.696
If you don't get
an assignment,


00:10:36.720 --> 00:10:37.789
you don't have a job.


00:10:39.080 --> 00:10:40.149
Eleanor.


00:10:40.240 --> 00:10:41.912
Data Reduction, building 202.


00:10:42.120 --> 00:10:43.235
Data Reduction?


00:10:43.400 --> 00:10:44.958
You're welcome
to join the charmers


00:10:45.080 --> 00:10:46.593
in Magnetoplasma Dynamics.


00:10:46.760 --> 00:10:47.909
Oh, no, ma'am.


00:10:48.120 --> 00:10:50.475
Thank you.
Data Reduction is just fine.


00:10:50.640 --> 00:10:52.153
Mm-hmm.


00:10:53.240 --> 00:10:54.559
Mary.
Present.


00:10:54.720 --> 00:10:57.154
The Mercury Seven prototype
has arrived for testing.


00:10:57.320 --> 00:10:59.276
Mr. Zielinski's
requesting you


00:11:00.160 --> 00:11:01.275
for permanent assignment.


00:11:01.440 --> 00:11:02.759
Are you serious?


00:11:02.920 --> 00:11:03.920
Get moving.


00:11:06.000 --> 00:11:08.116
Ooh, thank you, Jesus.


00:11:08.280 --> 00:11:09.395
Thank you, Jesus!


00:11:09.800 --> 00:11:11.995
Oh, thank you. Mary, Mary.
Keep it inside.


00:11:12.920 --> 00:11:14.512
Okay. I'm sorry, Dor.


00:11:16.920 --> 00:11:18.273
All right, Ms. Late.


00:11:18.440 --> 00:11:20.476
You will also be supporting
the east group.


00:11:21.840 --> 00:11:23.319
Mrs. Mitchell.


00:11:23.480 --> 00:11:25.436
We're on double-time
this morning, girls.


00:11:25.600 --> 00:11:27.909
I need these calculations
before lunch.


00:11:28.600 --> 00:11:31.353
Space Task Group
needs a computer, ASAP.


00:11:31.800 --> 00:11:34.109
Someone with a handle
on analytic geometry.


00:11:34.880 --> 00:11:37.110
We can't fill that position
out of the east group.


00:11:37.280 --> 00:11:38.429
Permanent or temp?


00:11:38.600 --> 00:11:40.955
Everything's temporary, Dorothy.


00:11:41.120 --> 00:11:42.155
You have someone?


00:11:42.320 --> 00:11:43.514
Yes, ma'am.


00:11:43.720 --> 00:11:45.631
Katherine's
the gal for that.


00:11:45.800 --> 00:11:47.950
She can handle any numbers
you put in front of her.


00:11:48.120 --> 00:11:49.439
I'll check her credentials.


00:11:51.800 --> 00:11:53.950
Didn't think I'd come
all the way down here.


00:11:54.120 --> 00:11:55.838
Here. Finish passing
those out.


00:11:56.000 --> 00:11:57.000
Yes, ma'am.


00:12:02.040 --> 00:12:03.598
Mrs. Mitchell.


00:12:03.760 --> 00:12:04.760
If I could...


00:12:06.320 --> 00:12:08.709
My application
for supervisor, ma'am.


00:12:08.880 --> 00:12:10.656
Was just wondering if they're
still considering me


00:12:10.680 --> 00:12:12.033
for that position.


00:12:12.200 --> 00:12:13.269
Yes.


00:12:13.440 --> 00:12:15.510
Well, the official
word is no.


00:12:16.120 --> 00:12:18.760
They're not assigning a permanent
supervisor for the colored group.


00:12:19.560 --> 00:12:20.879
May I ask why?


00:12:21.040 --> 00:12:22.519
I don't know why.


00:12:22.720 --> 00:12:24.199
I didn't ask why.


00:12:24.360 --> 00:12:25.918
We need a supervisor, ma'am.


00:12:26.080 --> 00:12:28.071
We haven't had one
since Miss Jansen got sick.


00:12:28.240 --> 00:12:29.593
It's been almost a year.


00:12:29.760 --> 00:12:31.352
Things are working
just fine as is.


00:12:31.560 --> 00:12:34.313
I'm doing the work
of a supervisor.


00:12:34.640 --> 00:12:36.835
Well, that's NASA for you.


00:12:37.600 --> 00:12:40.319
Fast with rocket ships,
slow with advancement.


00:12:40.920 --> 00:12:43.150
Get those trajectory
calcs worked out.


00:12:43.360 --> 00:12:44.952
We need them
for the Redstone test.


00:13:18.080 --> 00:13:20.833
Mach one tunnel test.
T-minus one minute.


00:13:33.520 --> 00:13:35.476
No shoe is worth your life.


00:13:35.640 --> 00:13:36.789
One moment.


00:13:37.680 --> 00:13:39.875
T-minus 10, 9...


00:13:40.040 --> 00:13:41.314
8...


00:13:41.400 --> 00:13:42.400
7...


00:13:42.440 --> 00:13:45.273
6, 5, 4...


00:14:11.760 --> 00:14:12.954
Shut it down.


00:14:19.120 --> 00:14:23.033
If we alter the exterior
from smooth to corrugated...


00:14:23.200 --> 00:14:25.794
the capsule might have
more stability.


00:14:25.960 --> 00:14:29.236
But the friction,
it is under during re-entry...


00:14:29.400 --> 00:14:32.597
Most shield erosion occurs


00:14:32.760 --> 00:14:35.672
on the posterior side
abutting the retro-boosters.


00:14:36.640 --> 00:14:37.834
Conclusion?


00:14:40.640 --> 00:14:42.358
The area closest to the boosters


00:14:42.440 --> 00:14:43.793
is closest to the heat.


00:14:44.680 --> 00:14:46.910
Coupled with rising
temperature on re-entry...


00:14:47.080 --> 00:14:48.672
the contact softens.


00:14:49.840 --> 00:14:51.956
We could consider
another fastener


00:14:52.040 --> 00:14:53.359
other than the bolts.


00:14:55.480 --> 00:14:56.549
Yes.


00:14:57.840 --> 00:14:58.955
Yes.


00:15:04.480 --> 00:15:05.629
There is another opening


00:15:05.720 --> 00:15:07.517
in the Engineer
Training Program.


00:15:08.520 --> 00:15:10.875
Flat head rivets
would reduce wind drag.


00:15:11.040 --> 00:15:13.759
Mary, a person
with engineer's mind


00:15:13.840 --> 00:15:15.353
should be an engineer.


00:15:15.560 --> 00:15:17.360
You can't be a computer
the rest of your life.


00:15:17.520 --> 00:15:18.669
Mr. Zielinski...


00:15:19.400 --> 00:15:20.992
I'm a Negro woman.


00:15:21.160 --> 00:15:22.991
I'm not gonna entertain
the impossible.


00:15:23.160 --> 00:15:24.639
And I'm a Polish Jew


00:15:24.720 --> 00:15:26.711
whose parents died
in a Nazi prison camp.


00:15:26.880 --> 00:15:29.917
Now I'm standing
beneath a spaceship


00:15:30.080 --> 00:15:33.038
that's going to carry an
astronaut to the stars.


00:15:33.240 --> 00:15:36.676
I think we can say we are
living the impossible.


00:15:37.480 --> 00:15:39.198
Let me ask you...


00:15:39.360 --> 00:15:40.793
if you were a white male,


00:15:41.040 --> 00:15:42.393
would you wish
to be an engineer?


00:15:43.680 --> 00:15:46.956
I wouldn't have to.
I'd already be one.


00:15:47.520 --> 00:15:49.351
Skirts must be worn
past the knee.


00:15:49.520 --> 00:15:51.715
Sweaters are preferred
to blouses.


00:15:51.880 --> 00:15:52.915
No jewelry.


00:15:53.000 --> 00:15:55.116
A simple pearl necklace
is the exception.


00:15:55.280 --> 00:15:58.033
Your supervisor
is Mr. Al Harrison,


00:15:58.200 --> 00:16:00.350
Director of
the Space Task Group.


00:16:00.560 --> 00:16:03.791
You'll write research,
proof calculations, so forth.


00:16:03.960 --> 00:16:06.758
Do not talk to Mr. Harrison
unless he talks to you.


00:16:07.600 --> 00:16:10.034
Not many computers last
more than a few days.


00:16:10.200 --> 00:16:12.236
He's been through a dozen
in as many months.


00:16:13.040 --> 00:16:14.234
Come on, keep up.


00:16:14.720 --> 00:16:16.233
Things move fast
around here.


00:16:17.560 --> 00:16:18.879
Your clearance.


00:16:20.240 --> 00:16:22.834
They've never had a colored
in here before, Katherine.


00:16:23.040 --> 00:16:24.189
Don't embarrass me.


00:16:50.360 --> 00:16:51.679
This wasn't emptied
last night.


00:16:51.840 --> 00:16:53.558
I'm sorry, I'm not the...


00:16:54.560 --> 00:16:55.754
custodian.


00:17:11.360 --> 00:17:12.429
Excuse me, ma'am.


00:17:12.600 --> 00:17:14.636
Mr. Harrison's computer
reporting.


00:17:14.840 --> 00:17:17.479
Take the desk in the back.
I'll get you work in a bit.


00:17:20.840 --> 00:17:24.037
Mr. Harrison won't warm up
to you. Don't expect it.


00:17:24.200 --> 00:17:25.520
Do your work,
keep your head down.


00:17:26.280 --> 00:17:27.280
Thank you.


00:17:27.760 --> 00:17:29.512
Go on. Get settled.


00:17:40.840 --> 00:17:41.840
Paul.


00:17:42.520 --> 00:17:44.238
Why are we still
losing shingles


00:17:44.360 --> 00:17:45.554
off the heat shield?


00:17:45.720 --> 00:17:48.075
We're working with a prototype
of the capsule, Al.


00:17:48.240 --> 00:17:50.056
Its lid is one third the
size of what it's going...


00:17:50.080 --> 00:17:51.080
I get that.


00:17:51.840 --> 00:17:54.673
So, what do we do with our
million dollar fan, Karl?


00:17:54.880 --> 00:17:57.110
Turn it down?
No, Al. I don't think...


00:17:57.600 --> 00:17:58.669
It was a joke.


00:18:00.120 --> 00:18:01.917
Paul, if the heat shield
comes off...


00:18:02.080 --> 00:18:03.760
what do you think happens
to our astronaut?


00:18:03.840 --> 00:18:04.840
I know.


00:18:06.400 --> 00:18:08.080
So we're gonna come up
with a solve, right?


00:18:08.160 --> 00:18:09.320
Yes, sir.
We're working on it.


00:18:09.960 --> 00:18:11.678
Ruth, what's the status
on that computer?


00:18:11.840 --> 00:18:13.831
She's right behind you,
Mr. Harrison.


00:18:18.960 --> 00:18:21.349
Does she handle
Analytic Geometry?


00:18:21.520 --> 00:18:24.398
Absolutely.
And she speaks.


00:18:24.560 --> 00:18:25.788
Yes, sir. I do.


00:18:26.920 --> 00:18:28.558
Which one?
Both.


00:18:28.720 --> 00:18:30.199
Geometry and speaking.


00:18:32.320 --> 00:18:34.436
Ruth, get me the...


00:18:37.560 --> 00:18:38.560
You think you...


00:18:39.560 --> 00:18:41.056
You think you can find me
the Frenet frame


00:18:41.080 --> 00:18:42.080
for this data?


00:18:42.240 --> 00:18:43.514
Using the Gram-Schmidt...


00:18:43.640 --> 00:18:45.676
Orthogonalization algorithm.
Yes, sir.


00:18:45.840 --> 00:18:48.479
I prefer it over
Euclidean coordinates.


00:18:51.720 --> 00:18:53.039
Okay, good.


00:18:54.120 --> 00:18:56.120
Good, then I'm gonna need
it by the end of the day.


00:18:56.160 --> 00:18:57.275
Yes.


00:18:57.440 --> 00:18:58.589
I'm also gonna ask you


00:18:58.680 --> 00:18:59.760
to check
Mr. Stafford's math


00:18:59.920 --> 00:19:01.273
as well as others' on this floor


00:19:01.360 --> 00:19:02.429
from time to time.


00:19:02.600 --> 00:19:03.600
I can handle that, Al.


00:19:03.760 --> 00:19:05.637
I'm sure you can, Paul.
I'm sure they all can.


00:19:05.800 --> 00:19:07.279
But if that were the case


00:19:07.440 --> 00:19:08.976
shingles wouldn't be flying
off the heat shield,


00:19:09.000 --> 00:19:10.000
now would they?


00:19:12.080 --> 00:19:13.877
Excuse me, could I have
everyone's attention


00:19:13.960 --> 00:19:15.029
for a moment?


00:19:17.160 --> 00:19:18.639
Thank you.


00:19:19.480 --> 00:19:20.799
Just to be clear...


00:19:20.960 --> 00:19:23.838
in 14 days, the Mercury Seven
will be here for training.


00:19:24.000 --> 00:19:26.833
And I have no doubt in my mind
that they're going to be


00:19:27.000 --> 00:19:28.831
asking us questions
about our work.


00:19:29.720 --> 00:19:31.119
I think that's pretty reasonable


00:19:31.280 --> 00:19:33.236
given we're putting a human
on top of a missile,


00:19:33.440 --> 00:19:34.440
shooting him into space,


00:19:34.600 --> 00:19:35.953
and it's never been
done before.


00:19:37.680 --> 00:19:38.840
Because it's never been done,


00:19:38.920 --> 00:19:41.309
everything we do between now
and then is gonna matter.


00:19:42.960 --> 00:19:44.552
It's gonna matter
to their wives...


00:19:45.760 --> 00:19:47.273
to their children.


00:19:47.960 --> 00:19:50.080
I believe it's gonna matter
to the whole damn country.


00:19:51.560 --> 00:19:55.109
So this Space Task Group
will be as advertised.


00:19:55.280 --> 00:19:58.511
America's greatest engineering
and scientific minds


00:19:58.840 --> 00:20:00.239
are not gonna have a problem


00:20:00.320 --> 00:20:02.754
with having their work checked.
Are they, Paul?


00:20:03.160 --> 00:20:04.195
No, sir.


00:20:04.360 --> 00:20:06.271
Good. So let's have
an amen, damn it.


00:20:06.440 --> 00:20:07.440
Amen.


00:20:08.080 --> 00:20:10.150
Amen!


00:20:10.880 --> 00:20:11.949
All right.


00:20:40.520 --> 00:20:42.158
My numbers are spot on.


00:20:43.800 --> 00:20:46.075
I will double check them, sir.
No problem.


00:20:54.560 --> 00:20:55.560
Uh...


00:20:55.760 --> 00:20:57.776
I'm not gonna be able to...
Work on what you can read.


00:20:57.800 --> 00:21:00.553
The rest is classified.
You don't have clearance.


00:21:17.560 --> 00:21:19.915
These are the numbers
Mr. Harrison gave me.


00:21:20.080 --> 00:21:22.036
Yes, I'm absolutely...
Excuse me.


00:21:22.200 --> 00:21:23.679
May I ask
where the ladies' room is?


00:21:23.880 --> 00:21:26.348
Sorry. I have no idea
where your bathroom is.


00:21:27.160 --> 00:21:28.639
Yes, sir.


00:21:29.400 --> 00:21:31.197
Uh, yes, sir.


00:22:01.200 --> 00:22:03.794
Summertime in
Virginia was an oven


00:22:04.960 --> 00:22:07.110
All the kids eating
ice cream with their cousins


00:22:08.240 --> 00:22:10.834
I was studyin' while you
was playing the dozens


00:22:11.920 --> 00:22:14.514
Don't act like you was there
when you wasn't


00:22:14.600 --> 00:22:16.318
Runnin' from the man


00:22:16.400 --> 00:22:17.628
Runnin' from the badge


00:22:18.880 --> 00:22:21.155
Don't act like you was there
when you wasn't


00:22:21.520 --> 00:22:23.158
Runnin' toward our plans


00:22:23.240 --> 00:22:24.673
And the judges hands


00:22:25.800 --> 00:22:27.836
Don't act like you was there
when you wasn't


00:22:28.920 --> 00:22:31.514
I know they say
you crawl before you walk


00:22:32.360 --> 00:22:35.113
But in my mind I already jog


00:22:35.760 --> 00:22:38.638
If I stand still
I cannot get far


00:22:39.320 --> 00:22:41.436
They want the moon
I'm on Mars


00:22:41.520 --> 00:22:46.833
Sometime my mind dives deep
when I'm runnin'


00:22:48.560 --> 00:22:53.588
Together we'll fly
But no more runnin'


00:22:55.040 --> 00:22:56.040
Ruth!


00:22:56.200 --> 00:22:57.200
Yes, Mr. Harrison?


00:22:57.800 --> 00:22:59.995
Ruth, where's that girl
with the numbers?


00:23:00.160 --> 00:23:02.276
On a... break.


00:23:03.400 --> 00:23:04.435
We take breaks now?


00:23:06.720 --> 00:23:11.714
Sometime my mind dives deep
When I'm runnin'


00:23:12.840 --> 00:23:15.400
I don't want no free ride


00:23:16.160 --> 00:23:18.799
I'm just sick and tired
of runnin'


00:23:20.240 --> 00:23:25.075
Together we fly
But no more runnin'


00:23:32.320 --> 00:23:33.514
My God, where have you been?


00:23:33.720 --> 00:23:34.869
Are you finished yet?


00:23:35.040 --> 00:23:36.075
Uh...


00:23:36.160 --> 00:23:37.832
Almost. He said
by the end of the day.


00:23:37.920 --> 00:23:39.797
The end of the day around
here was yesterday.


00:23:41.200 --> 00:23:42.474
I need those done first.


00:23:42.640 --> 00:23:43.993
He wants those done first.
Okay.


00:23:44.160 --> 00:23:45.160
Get going.


00:23:45.360 --> 00:23:46.429
I'm sorry about that.


00:23:47.360 --> 00:23:48.839
Somebody's working on it
right now.


00:24:54.680 --> 00:24:55.749
Mr. Harrison.


00:24:57.600 --> 00:24:59.591
Yeah. Uh, Just set it...
You know.


00:25:02.680 --> 00:25:04.830
And Stafford's
heat shield calcs?


00:25:06.240 --> 00:25:07.992
It's hard
to be sure, sir.


00:25:08.800 --> 00:25:10.153
Hard to be sure.


00:25:11.000 --> 00:25:12.638
You know
what we're doing here?


00:25:14.280 --> 00:25:16.191
Trying to put
a man into space, sir.


00:25:16.360 --> 00:25:17.554
That's right.


00:25:18.560 --> 00:25:20.480
That's right. So you can
throw that in the trash.


00:25:20.640 --> 00:25:21.914
Excuse me?


00:25:22.640 --> 00:25:24.596
I said you can throw it away.


00:25:25.120 --> 00:25:26.120
Here.


00:25:27.240 --> 00:25:28.719
It's not an insult
to your work.


00:25:28.920 --> 00:25:30.433
It's just obsolete.


00:25:30.600 --> 00:25:32.830
That's how fast things
are moving around here.


00:25:33.000 --> 00:25:35.355
If I said I was sorry,
I'd be saying it all day.


00:25:36.240 --> 00:25:37.309
What I'm asking you to do,


00:25:37.400 --> 00:25:38.960
what I'm asking everyone
in that room...


00:25:39.120 --> 00:25:41.190
all my geniuses...


00:25:41.360 --> 00:25:43.032
is to look
beyond the numbers.


00:25:43.920 --> 00:25:45.876
To look around them.


00:25:46.040 --> 00:25:47.678
Through them.


00:25:47.840 --> 00:25:50.070
For answers to questions
we don't even know to ask.


00:25:50.880 --> 00:25:52.916
Math that
doesn't yet exist.


00:25:53.520 --> 00:25:55.829
Because without it,
we're not going anywhere.


00:25:56.000 --> 00:25:57.752
We're staying on the ground.


00:25:57.920 --> 00:26:00.480
We're not flying into space.
We're not circling the Earth.


00:26:00.640 --> 00:26:02.358
And we're certainly not
touching the Moon.


00:26:02.520 --> 00:26:03.714
And in my mind...


00:26:04.920 --> 00:26:07.275
In my mind, I'm already there.


00:26:09.520 --> 00:26:10.520
Are you?


00:26:12.080 --> 00:26:13.229
Yes, sir.


00:26:14.160 --> 00:26:15.388
Good.


00:26:15.560 --> 00:26:16.936
Because I don't need
another smart girl


00:26:16.960 --> 00:26:18.188
with an adding machine.


00:26:21.520 --> 00:26:22.873
Can I go, sir?


00:26:26.240 --> 00:26:27.958
Yeah, go.


00:26:29.240 --> 00:26:30.639
Yes, you can go.


00:27:01.920 --> 00:27:05.151
We've been waiting for hours.
Next time, you take the bus.


00:27:05.320 --> 00:27:06.548
She's in a mood.


00:27:09.960 --> 00:27:11.837
People talkin'
tryin' to break us up


00:27:12.240 --> 00:27:15.118
Why won't they let us be?


00:27:15.240 --> 00:27:17.754
Brick and stones
may break my bones


00:27:17.840 --> 00:27:19.831
But talk don't bother me


00:27:19.920 --> 00:27:23.037
I've been abused


00:27:23.160 --> 00:27:24.559
In my heart


00:27:25.200 --> 00:27:27.555
I haven't been late
one day in 10 years.


00:27:27.720 --> 00:27:30.359
Haven't been out sick.
Haven't complained.


00:27:30.520 --> 00:27:31.520
My work's on time.


00:27:31.600 --> 00:27:32.999
It's done right.
It's done well.


00:27:33.160 --> 00:27:34.513
It's not fair,
that's a fact.


00:27:34.680 --> 00:27:36.193
What's not fair
is having


00:27:36.280 --> 00:27:37.416
the responsibility
of a supervisor...


00:27:37.440 --> 00:27:39.237
- but not the title or the pay.
- Mmm.


00:27:40.080 --> 00:27:41.229
Watching you two move on.


00:27:42.200 --> 00:27:43.997
Now, don't get me wrong.


00:27:44.160 --> 00:27:46.720
Any upward movement
is movement for us all.


00:27:48.080 --> 00:27:49.877
Just isn't movement for me.


00:27:50.040 --> 00:27:51.040
Truth be told, Dorothy...


00:27:51.200 --> 00:27:53.430
I don't even know
if I can keep up in that room.


00:27:54.360 --> 00:27:56.874
I'll be back with
the computers in a week.


00:27:57.040 --> 00:27:58.792
Or out of a job entirely.


00:27:58.960 --> 00:27:59.995
Oh, please.


00:28:00.160 --> 00:28:01.816
You're better with the numbers
than anyone in there,


00:28:01.840 --> 00:28:02.920
Katherine, and you know it.


00:28:03.000 --> 00:28:05.136
Just make that pencil move
as fast as your mind does...


00:28:05.160 --> 00:28:06.639
you'll be fine.


00:28:06.800 --> 00:28:08.677
And you,
have some respect.


00:28:08.840 --> 00:28:10.478
Get your damn feet
off my dashboard.


00:28:10.560 --> 00:28:11.680
This isn't your living room.


00:28:16.040 --> 00:28:17.792
I sound like a supervisor,
don't I?


00:28:17.960 --> 00:28:19.518
A mean old salty one.


00:28:19.680 --> 00:28:21.511
Riddled with authority,
no question.


00:28:23.000 --> 00:28:24.752
You don't mess
with Mrs. Vaughan.


00:28:26.040 --> 00:28:27.439
Turn that music up.


00:28:43.920 --> 00:28:45.120
Good night,
Katherine.


00:28:45.240 --> 00:28:46.240
Bye, Kat.


00:28:46.320 --> 00:28:47.320
Good night, ladies.


00:28:47.400 --> 00:28:48.720
- See you in the morning.
- Bye.


00:28:57.560 --> 00:28:58.560
Hey, baby.


00:28:58.720 --> 00:28:59.789
Hello, Mama.


00:29:00.400 --> 00:29:03.119
I was fixing to call
the National Guard.


00:29:04.280 --> 00:29:07.909
Sorry, Mama.
New assignment.


00:29:08.080 --> 00:29:09.877
Space Task Group.


00:29:10.040 --> 00:29:12.031
Oh, my word.
That sounds important.


00:29:12.200 --> 00:29:13.872
It's a promotion?


00:29:14.200 --> 00:29:16.873
It might be
a slight promotion.


00:29:17.680 --> 00:29:18.999
But you want the job, right?


00:29:20.920 --> 00:29:22.478
I think so.


00:29:22.640 --> 00:29:24.392
It's a challenge, but...


00:29:24.520 --> 00:29:26.636
It's nothing you
can't handle, Katherine.


00:29:28.600 --> 00:29:30.238
Thank you, Mama.


00:29:31.840 --> 00:29:32.909
Are they asleep yet?


00:29:33.120 --> 00:29:34.712
They're pretending to be.


00:29:34.880 --> 00:29:37.030
- What are you doing?
- I don't...


00:29:37.200 --> 00:29:38.872
They're not pretending
well enough.


00:29:38.960 --> 00:29:39.960
Constance!


00:29:40.040 --> 00:29:41.917
Thank you, Mama.
Love you.


00:29:42.080 --> 00:29:43.433
This is my bed.
No.


00:29:43.520 --> 00:29:44.520
Get off!


00:29:44.600 --> 00:29:45.999
Go back to yours!
I'm oldest! No.


00:29:46.080 --> 00:29:47.376
- What are you doing?
- Get off!


00:29:47.400 --> 00:29:48.628
What are you doing?


00:29:48.800 --> 00:29:51.360
It's not fair how Joylette
gets to sleep by herself


00:29:51.560 --> 00:29:53.198
because she's the oldest.


00:29:53.360 --> 00:29:54.793
Yes, it is fair.
No, it's not.


00:29:54.960 --> 00:29:56.456
- Yes, it is.
- Nuh-uh.


00:29:56.480 --> 00:29:58.710
Constance, Kathy, sit.


00:30:02.880 --> 00:30:05.030
Now, I understand...


00:30:05.240 --> 00:30:07.913
you want to be grown
and have your own space.


00:30:08.800 --> 00:30:10.756
So, whoever sleeps
in that bed,


00:30:10.880 --> 00:30:12.472
in Joylette's place...


00:30:12.640 --> 00:30:14.392
will also do the dishes...


00:30:14.560 --> 00:30:16.312
take out the trash...


00:30:16.480 --> 00:30:18.436
and do all of
Joylette's chores.


00:30:19.120 --> 00:30:21.429
That's okay. Never mind.


00:30:21.600 --> 00:30:23.318
Yeah, she's not so
terrible to share with.


00:30:25.680 --> 00:30:26.680
Okay.


00:30:26.760 --> 00:30:28.159
Then don't let me
come home


00:30:28.240 --> 00:30:30.276
and find you like
this again, you hear me?


00:30:30.440 --> 00:30:32.908
You're supposed to been
asleep a long time ago.


00:30:33.080 --> 00:30:34.593
We just wanted
to say goodnight.


00:30:34.760 --> 00:30:37.320
You've been gone
for 300 hours.


00:30:39.600 --> 00:30:41.636
I know. I work a
full-time job now.


00:30:41.800 --> 00:30:43.313
You all know that.


00:30:45.280 --> 00:30:48.078
I have to be Mommy and Daddy.


00:30:52.520 --> 00:30:54.511
I miss your father
just as much as anyone.


00:30:55.280 --> 00:30:57.475
He's with the angels.


00:30:57.640 --> 00:30:59.596
Yes, he's with
all the angels.


00:30:59.760 --> 00:31:01.432
And he's watching over us.


00:31:01.600 --> 00:31:03.875
Every minute, okay?


00:31:04.040 --> 00:31:06.429
That's why we have
to be strong.


00:31:06.520 --> 00:31:07.520
All right?


00:31:07.600 --> 00:31:08.953
Yes, Mama.
Yes, Mama.


00:31:09.120 --> 00:31:10.473
Y'all get some rest, okay?


00:31:11.040 --> 00:31:12.040
Mama?


00:31:12.120 --> 00:31:13.120
Yes, baby?


00:31:13.720 --> 00:31:16.029
Are the Russians fixin'
to attack us?


00:31:16.640 --> 00:31:17.789
Where'd you
get that from?


00:31:17.960 --> 00:31:19.480
We had to hide under
our desks today...


00:31:20.320 --> 00:31:21.389
all scrunched up.


00:31:22.000 --> 00:31:24.036
Okay, baby,
that's called a drill.


00:31:24.200 --> 00:31:25.679
It means, uh...


00:31:25.840 --> 00:31:27.319
you practice safety


00:31:27.400 --> 00:31:29.789
just in case something happens.


00:31:32.400 --> 00:31:35.233
Truth is, we don't know
what they're doing up there.


00:31:35.400 --> 00:31:36.879
You going to space, too?


00:31:37.080 --> 00:31:38.479
No, baby.


00:31:38.640 --> 00:31:41.791
But I'm going to do
what I can


00:31:42.000 --> 00:31:44.992
to help those brave men
get there. Yes, I am.


00:31:45.160 --> 00:31:47.993
You could fly up to space
if you wanted to, Mama.


00:31:48.160 --> 00:31:49.639
You could be an astronaut.


00:31:49.840 --> 00:31:51.717
Did you draw this?
Yes, Mama.


00:31:53.040 --> 00:31:55.031
Is that me?


00:31:55.200 --> 00:31:56.200
Mmm-hmm.


00:31:56.920 --> 00:31:58.069
You see this?


00:31:59.560 --> 00:32:01.152
Oh, my goodness.


00:32:01.320 --> 00:32:02.912
I love you, baby.


00:32:04.000 --> 00:32:05.000
I know.


00:32:05.160 --> 00:32:06.559
Come here.


00:32:06.720 --> 00:32:09.075
All right. Now this time
really go to bed.


00:32:09.680 --> 00:32:10.680
Okay?


00:32:10.840 --> 00:32:12.558
I love y'all.
Good night.


00:32:12.720 --> 00:32:13.840
Good night.
Good night, Mama.


00:32:13.880 --> 00:32:15.836
I love my picture, baby.


00:32:24.600 --> 00:32:25.715
Thank you, Lord.


00:32:28.240 --> 00:32:30.071
- Praise God!
- Praise God!


00:32:30.240 --> 00:32:32.356
Hallelujah!
Hallelujah!


00:32:34.520 --> 00:32:35.520
Hallelujah.


00:32:36.960 --> 00:32:38.359
Praise God...


00:32:39.360 --> 00:32:42.079
that change is coming.


00:32:44.080 --> 00:32:47.390
From the Southern Christian
Leadership Conference...


00:32:47.560 --> 00:32:49.551
to the good reverend
Dr. King...


00:32:49.720 --> 00:32:51.039
Amen!
Hallelujah!


00:32:51.200 --> 00:32:53.395
...to the students sitting in
at lunch counters


00:32:53.480 --> 00:32:54.480
in North Carolina.


00:32:54.640 --> 00:32:55.640
That's right.
Yes, sir.


00:32:55.760 --> 00:32:58.593
His faith in us
has no limit.


00:32:59.320 --> 00:33:02.596
The good Lord
has our very own women


00:33:02.760 --> 00:33:04.398
working on rocket ships.


00:33:08.560 --> 00:33:11.597
And we have National Guardsmen


00:33:11.800 --> 00:33:12.949
defending our freedom.


00:33:13.120 --> 00:33:15.395
Give a shout of praise
for Colonel Jim Johnson.


00:33:18.160 --> 00:33:20.071
Returning home,
by the grace of God


00:33:20.240 --> 00:33:22.595
to set up
a National Guard unit


00:33:22.960 --> 00:33:24.234
right here in Hampton.


00:33:25.240 --> 00:33:26.673
Can I get a hallelujah in here?


00:33:26.840 --> 00:33:28.831
Hallelujah!


00:33:33.080 --> 00:33:34.479
I just don't understand.


00:33:35.280 --> 00:33:36.633
I don't understand it.


00:33:36.800 --> 00:33:38.440
I don't wanna talk
about this again, okay?


00:33:38.840 --> 00:33:40.193
A female engineer?


00:33:41.360 --> 00:33:42.360
A female engineer?


00:33:42.920 --> 00:33:44.035
We're Negro, baby.


00:33:44.120 --> 00:33:45.480
Ain't no such thing.
Understand it.


00:33:45.600 --> 00:33:46.953
It's not like that
there, Levi.


00:33:47.120 --> 00:33:48.439
You can't apply for freedom.


00:33:48.600 --> 00:33:51.319
Freedom is never granted
to the oppressed.


00:33:51.480 --> 00:33:52.993
It's got
to be demanded. Taken.


00:33:53.160 --> 00:33:54.991
Stop quoting your slogans at me.


00:33:55.520 --> 00:33:57.795
There is more than one way
to achieve something.


00:33:57.960 --> 00:33:58.960
I don't want any greens.


00:33:59.000 --> 00:34:00.956
Hey. I tell you
about interrupting?


00:34:01.040 --> 00:34:02.792
Here. Try this, baby.


00:34:04.880 --> 00:34:05.880
Yes.


00:34:05.960 --> 00:34:07.757
You're gonna eat
them greens, too.


00:34:07.840 --> 00:34:09.796
Kids need
to eat their vegetables.


00:34:09.960 --> 00:34:11.678
You'd know that
if you was ever home.


00:34:14.040 --> 00:34:15.951
You better settle down,
Levi Jackson.


00:34:16.120 --> 00:34:17.792
Unless you want
this female's mind,


00:34:17.880 --> 00:34:19.871
right here,
in front of everybody.


00:34:21.960 --> 00:34:23.393
All I'm saying...


00:34:23.560 --> 00:34:25.312
don't play the fool.


00:34:25.480 --> 00:34:27.311
I don't wanna
see you get hurt.


00:34:27.480 --> 00:34:29.391
NASA's never ever
given you guys your due.


00:34:29.560 --> 00:34:30.913
Having a couple
of extra degrees


00:34:31.000 --> 00:34:32.149
ain't gonna change that.


00:34:33.680 --> 00:34:35.830
Civil rights
ain't always civil.


00:34:41.480 --> 00:34:43.152
I don't want
any greens either.


00:34:43.320 --> 00:34:44.355
No lip, little miss.


00:34:44.560 --> 00:34:46.040
Your brother gets them,
and so do you.


00:34:49.040 --> 00:34:51.395
I hear he's planning
on staying in the area.


00:34:52.840 --> 00:34:54.751
Oh, yeah?
Well, that's good for him.


00:34:54.920 --> 00:34:57.388
Katherine, go find
your way over there.


00:34:57.560 --> 00:35:00.597
That Colonel Jim
is a tall glass of water.


00:35:00.760 --> 00:35:02.034
That he is.


00:35:02.240 --> 00:35:04.754
Tall, strong, commanding.


00:35:04.920 --> 00:35:07.195
And I bet he's like that
day and night.


00:35:07.360 --> 00:35:09.954
Mary, it's Sunday.


00:35:10.120 --> 00:35:11.439
Please have some shame.


00:35:11.600 --> 00:35:12.874
I will not.


00:35:13.600 --> 00:35:16.068
Hmm, I think he's
smiling over here.


00:35:16.240 --> 00:35:17.434
At you.


00:35:21.880 --> 00:35:23.108
Well I'm not smiling back.


00:35:23.200 --> 00:35:24.235
Yes, you are.
Oh, child.


00:35:24.320 --> 00:35:26.151
You are all teeth right now.
I am not.


00:35:27.360 --> 00:35:28.588
Come over here.


00:35:29.040 --> 00:35:30.080
He's coming over.


00:35:30.200 --> 00:35:31.576
Now, why would
he be doing that?


00:35:31.600 --> 00:35:33.280
- 'Cause Mary's waving at him.
- No!


00:35:33.400 --> 00:35:35.096
Ladies, I'm not ready.
It's too late. Fix your hair.


00:35:35.120 --> 00:35:36.120
Hello, Colonel.


00:35:37.200 --> 00:35:38.394
I'm Dorothy Vaughan.


00:35:38.560 --> 00:35:40.696
That's Mary Jackson.
I believe you met her husband, Levi.


00:35:40.720 --> 00:35:42.233
Yes, ma'am.
Good to meet y'all.


00:35:42.400 --> 00:35:43.753
And Mrs. Katherine Goble.


00:35:43.920 --> 00:35:45.717
She's not married.
She's a widow...


00:35:45.880 --> 00:35:48.075
...with three beautiful
little girls.


00:35:48.240 --> 00:35:49.240
So well behaved.


00:35:49.400 --> 00:35:51.550
Angels on earth is what
we like to call them.


00:35:51.720 --> 00:35:53.392
Dorothy, slice of pie?


00:35:53.560 --> 00:35:55.073
I'd love one.


00:35:55.240 --> 00:35:56.719
Excuse me.


00:35:56.920 --> 00:35:58.056
You already have
a slice of pie...


00:35:58.080 --> 00:35:59.080
Uh-huh.


00:36:02.760 --> 00:36:05.399
I was just, uh, fixing my
three babies some plates...


00:36:05.600 --> 00:36:06.794
Let me help you.


00:36:07.760 --> 00:36:08.988
Sure.


00:36:09.160 --> 00:36:11.151
Fruit salad.
Mmm-hmm.


00:36:12.640 --> 00:36:15.518
Pastor mentioned you're
a computer at NASA.


00:36:15.600 --> 00:36:16.600
Yes.


00:36:16.640 --> 00:36:17.993
What's that entail?


00:36:18.600 --> 00:36:19.600
Oh.


00:36:19.920 --> 00:36:21.911
We calculate
the mathematics necessary


00:36:22.080 --> 00:36:24.640
to enable launch and landing
for the space program.


00:36:25.000 --> 00:36:27.309
That's pretty heady stuff.


00:36:27.480 --> 00:36:28.515
Yes, it is.


00:36:28.680 --> 00:36:30.398
They let women
handle that sort of...


00:36:30.480 --> 00:36:31.480
Uh...


00:36:33.600 --> 00:36:35.989
That's not what I mean.


00:36:36.160 --> 00:36:37.479
What do you mean?


00:36:40.360 --> 00:36:43.591
I'm just surprised
that something so...


00:36:45.440 --> 00:36:46.714
taxing...
Mmm-hmm.


00:36:46.800 --> 00:36:48.313
Mr. Johnson.


00:36:49.440 --> 00:36:51.635
If I were you,
I'd quit talking right now.


00:36:51.800 --> 00:36:53.028
I didn't mean
no disrespect.


00:36:54.280 --> 00:36:55.554
I will have you know


00:36:55.720 --> 00:36:57.790
I was the first
Negro female student


00:36:57.960 --> 00:37:00.155
at West Virginia University
Graduate School.


00:37:00.880 --> 00:37:02.108
On any given day...


00:37:02.320 --> 00:37:04.390
I analyze the manometer levels


00:37:04.480 --> 00:37:06.948
for air displacement,
friction, and velocity


00:37:07.160 --> 00:37:09.594
and compute
over 10,000 calculations


00:37:09.680 --> 00:37:11.398
by cosine, square root...


00:37:11.560 --> 00:37:13.596
and, lately, analytic geometry.


00:37:13.680 --> 00:37:14.680
By hand.


00:37:15.200 --> 00:37:17.760
There are 20 bright,
highly capable


00:37:17.880 --> 00:37:19.677
Negro women
in the West Computing Group.


00:37:19.880 --> 00:37:22.314
And we're proud to be doing
our part for the country.


00:37:23.160 --> 00:37:24.160
So, yes.


00:37:24.320 --> 00:37:26.709
They let women do some things
at NASA, Mr. Johnson.


00:37:27.400 --> 00:37:29.675
And it's not because
we wear skirts.


00:37:30.240 --> 00:37:32.231
It's because
we wear glasses.


00:37:33.400 --> 00:37:34.879
Have a good day.


00:37:48.920 --> 00:37:50.194
Scott, welcome to Langley.


00:37:51.560 --> 00:37:52.675
Welcome to Langley.
Welcome.


00:37:53.520 --> 00:37:55.192
Good to have
you here, gentlemen.


00:38:04.280 --> 00:38:05.554
Thank you.


00:38:05.760 --> 00:38:08.399
That's Alan Shepard,
U.S. Navy pilot.


00:38:08.560 --> 00:38:10.391
He could be the
first man in space.


00:38:10.600 --> 00:38:13.239
And that's Scott Carpenter
and Walter Schirra...


00:38:13.400 --> 00:38:14.958
also Navy pilots.


00:38:15.120 --> 00:38:17.873
All under 5'11", 180 pounds.


00:38:18.040 --> 00:38:19.632
IQs over 130.
Mmm.


00:38:19.800 --> 00:38:21.711
And handsome must be
a requirement too.


00:38:21.880 --> 00:38:24.599
How could you possibly be
ogling these white men?


00:38:24.760 --> 00:38:25.760
It's equal rights.


00:38:25.880 --> 00:38:27.871
I have the right to see
fine in every color.


00:38:28.880 --> 00:38:32.190
And that's John Glenn.
He's the only Marine Corps pilot.


00:38:32.760 --> 00:38:34.876
Gentlemen, if we may,
much to see around here.


00:38:35.080 --> 00:38:36.960
Well, heck, we haven't
shaken all the hands yet.


00:38:37.000 --> 00:38:38.640
We have a fairly tight
itinerary, Colonel.


00:38:38.720 --> 00:38:39.720
Ruth, he's fine.


00:38:39.920 --> 00:38:41.216
He's coming this way.
Look straight ahead.


00:38:41.240 --> 00:38:42.240
I'm looking right at him.


00:38:42.400 --> 00:38:45.392
Ladies, I didn't wanna run
off without saying hello.


00:38:45.560 --> 00:38:47.073
Seem to be in a big
rush around here.


00:38:47.240 --> 00:38:49.151
The Russians certainly
aren't slowing down any.


00:38:49.320 --> 00:38:50.912
They can't build
a damn refrigerator.


00:38:51.000 --> 00:38:52.600
How the heck did they
beat us into space?


00:38:53.920 --> 00:38:55.216
And what do you ladies do
for NASA?


00:38:55.240 --> 00:38:57.800
Calculate your trajectories.
Launch and landing.


00:38:57.920 --> 00:38:58.976
You can't get anywhere
without the numbers.


00:38:59.000 --> 00:39:00.000
No, sir.


00:39:00.600 --> 00:39:03.956
Mary Jackson, Mr. Glenn.
Engineering.


00:39:04.120 --> 00:39:05.576
And I'm proud as the devil
to be working with you.


00:39:05.600 --> 00:39:06.749
Thank you, Mary.


00:39:06.840 --> 00:39:07.840
John.


00:39:09.200 --> 00:39:10.758
John, let's take it inside.
Yes, sir.


00:39:10.920 --> 00:39:12.558
Guys.
Nice meeting you guys.


00:39:12.840 --> 00:39:14.200
Thank you, Colonel.
Good luck, sir.


00:39:14.280 --> 00:39:15.599
God bless you all.


00:39:16.600 --> 00:39:17.794
Thank you. That's wonderful.


00:39:18.600 --> 00:39:19.600
Thank you!


00:39:50.120 --> 00:39:51.519
Mr. Harrison wants you
to confirm


00:39:51.680 --> 00:39:54.319
launch and landing for the
Redstone rocket test.


00:39:59.320 --> 00:40:02.551
I cannot work on what
I cannot see, Mr. Stafford.


00:40:02.720 --> 00:40:03.789
It's illegible.


00:40:04.000 --> 00:40:05.600
Those numbers have
already been confirmed


00:40:05.680 --> 00:40:07.557
by two engineers in this
department and myself.


00:40:07.640 --> 00:40:09.756
This is more or less
a dummy check.


00:40:46.360 --> 00:40:48.360
You have one, we make it two.
Make it double, okay?


00:40:48.400 --> 00:40:50.197
We gotta get it.
Just make it happen.


00:40:53.040 --> 00:40:55.235
No one thought to measure
these things?


00:40:55.400 --> 00:40:57.038
I'm afraid not, sir.


00:40:58.440 --> 00:41:00.556
Well, would you
like us to stop?


00:41:00.720 --> 00:41:02.711
No. No, just...


00:41:03.360 --> 00:41:05.794
Actually, no.
Just keep going.


00:41:06.800 --> 00:41:09.439
In fact, use the
big hammer.


00:41:09.600 --> 00:41:10.600
Yes, sir.


00:41:10.720 --> 00:41:12.856
Come on, use the big hammer.
You heard the man. Let's go.


00:41:12.880 --> 00:41:13.880
Watch out.


00:41:39.920 --> 00:41:40.920
Ma'am.


00:41:43.280 --> 00:41:45.510
Our runner can pick up
your work, Dorothy.


00:41:46.480 --> 00:41:48.152
You need to be working,
not walking.


00:41:48.320 --> 00:41:51.596
Oh, it's break time, ma'am.
No trouble at all.


00:41:51.760 --> 00:41:53.432
Since you made the trip...


00:41:53.600 --> 00:41:55.716
you can take that batch
over there.


00:41:56.480 --> 00:41:58.277
Sure thing, Mrs. Mitchell.


00:42:01.280 --> 00:42:03.475
You need something else?
Yes, ma'am.


00:42:04.320 --> 00:42:05.976
I was wondering if you know
what they're building


00:42:06.000 --> 00:42:07.000
over in the tech wing.


00:42:09.880 --> 00:42:11.950
It's called an IBM.


00:42:12.120 --> 00:42:13.599
A mainframe machine.


00:42:14.320 --> 00:42:15.833
Apparently, it can
do our calculations


00:42:15.960 --> 00:42:17.000
in a fraction of the time.


00:42:17.680 --> 00:42:18.680
Hmm.


00:42:19.280 --> 00:42:20.952
I guess that's good for NASA.


00:42:24.360 --> 00:42:26.476
Hurry on, now.
Best if you do it in one trip.


00:42:44.160 --> 00:42:45.229
Excuse me.


00:43:13.440 --> 00:43:14.440
Hi.


00:43:20.000 --> 00:43:21.752
What are you
looking at, Paul?


00:43:30.080 --> 00:43:31.513
Is that correct?


00:43:32.960 --> 00:43:33.960
Yeah.


00:43:40.760 --> 00:43:41.795
Whose work is that?


00:43:44.040 --> 00:43:45.871
I said, whose work is that?


00:43:48.880 --> 00:43:50.472
Katherine Goble, sir.


00:43:54.360 --> 00:43:55.360
Hello.
Hi.


00:43:55.400 --> 00:43:56.456
How did you know
the Redstone


00:43:56.480 --> 00:43:58.038
couldn't support orbital flight?


00:43:58.200 --> 00:43:59.376
That's classified information.


00:43:59.400 --> 00:44:00.400
It's top secret.


00:44:01.040 --> 00:44:03.235
It's no secret why the
Redstone tests keep failing.


00:44:03.400 --> 00:44:04.879
It's fine
for sub-orbital flight


00:44:05.040 --> 00:44:07.076
but it can't handle
the weight of the capsule


00:44:07.240 --> 00:44:08.240
and push it into space.


00:44:08.880 --> 00:44:10.393
Numbers don't lie.


00:44:11.320 --> 00:44:13.311
And you figured
all that out with this?


00:44:15.080 --> 00:44:16.911
Half the data is redacted.


00:44:17.800 --> 00:44:21.156
But what's there tells a story
if you read between the lines.


00:44:21.320 --> 00:44:23.000
The distance from launch
to orbit, we know.


00:44:23.160 --> 00:44:24.354
Redstone mass, we know.


00:44:24.440 --> 00:44:26.396
Mercury capsule weight, we know.


00:44:26.600 --> 00:44:29.398
And the speeds are there
in the data.


00:44:29.600 --> 00:44:31.238
You did the math.


00:44:31.640 --> 00:44:32.640
Yes, sir.


00:44:33.440 --> 00:44:35.271
I looked beyond.


00:44:37.440 --> 00:44:39.749
How do you know
about the Atlas rocket?


00:44:42.760 --> 00:44:44.751
That's not math.


00:44:44.920 --> 00:44:45.989
That data is not here.


00:44:46.120 --> 00:44:47.792
Like you said, it's classified.


00:44:53.960 --> 00:44:55.757
I held it up to the light.


00:44:55.960 --> 00:44:57.279
You held it up
to the light?


00:44:57.440 --> 00:44:58.509
Yes, sir.


00:45:03.800 --> 00:45:04.800
Well, there it is.


00:45:04.960 --> 00:45:05.960
Mmm-hmm.


00:45:07.280 --> 00:45:08.280
Atlas.


00:45:09.200 --> 00:45:10.269
What's your name?


00:45:10.440 --> 00:45:11.953
Katherine Goble.


00:45:13.280 --> 00:45:14.633
Are you a spy, Katherine?


00:45:14.800 --> 00:45:15.800
Am I what?


00:45:15.840 --> 00:45:17.040
I said, are you a Russian spy?


00:45:17.120 --> 00:45:18.473
No, sir.


00:45:18.640 --> 00:45:19.993
I'm not Russian.


00:45:21.520 --> 00:45:23.158
She's not Russian, sir.


00:45:24.960 --> 00:45:26.791
All right then, we have
nothing to lose here.


00:45:26.960 --> 00:45:28.016
Give her everything she needs


00:45:28.040 --> 00:45:29.296
to work on Shepard's
trajectories.


00:45:29.320 --> 00:45:31.390
Without redaction.
Are we clear on that?


00:45:31.560 --> 00:45:32.834
Are we sure about this?


00:45:33.040 --> 00:45:34.040
What's the issue, Paul?


00:45:34.200 --> 00:45:35.633
You heard her.


00:45:35.800 --> 00:45:36.800
She's not a spy.


00:45:37.640 --> 00:45:39.232
I just don't think
it's a good idea.


00:45:39.400 --> 00:45:43.075
You know what I think's
a good idea? Darker ink.


00:45:43.240 --> 00:45:44.520
I think darker ink's
a good idea.


00:45:44.640 --> 00:45:45.736
Ruth, could you
pass it along?


00:45:45.760 --> 00:45:46.760
Yes, sir.


00:45:46.920 --> 00:45:47.989
Thank you, Katherine.


00:45:48.160 --> 00:45:49.309
Thank you, sir.


00:45:55.840 --> 00:45:57.831
This interrogation's over.


00:46:16.400 --> 00:46:18.550
You can probably get to the
moon and back with this.


00:46:18.720 --> 00:46:20.119
Just not the bathroom.


00:46:20.280 --> 00:46:22.430
At least they're not
blind of what you do.


00:46:22.600 --> 00:46:23.680
It's not gonna matter soon.


00:46:23.880 --> 00:46:26.474
This IBM's gonna
put us all out of work.


00:46:26.640 --> 00:46:28.551
How you doing?
What can we do about it?


00:46:29.280 --> 00:46:31.271
Only one thing to do.


00:46:31.440 --> 00:46:33.158
Learn all we can,
make ourselves valuable.


00:46:33.760 --> 00:46:35.216
Somewhere down the line,
a human being


00:46:35.240 --> 00:46:36.536
is gonna have
to push the buttons.


00:46:36.560 --> 00:46:37.959
That's right.


00:46:40.160 --> 00:46:41.718
Oh, mercy.


00:46:41.880 --> 00:46:43.472
Well, she must be lost.


00:46:43.640 --> 00:46:44.856
NASA doesn't commission females


00:46:44.880 --> 00:46:46.200
for the Engineer
Training Program.


00:46:47.400 --> 00:46:51.109
That position is available
to any qualified applicant.


00:46:51.280 --> 00:46:54.078
Right. Except, you don't have
the educational requirements.


00:46:54.240 --> 00:46:55.309
I have a bachelor's degree


00:46:55.400 --> 00:46:56.736
in mathematics
and physical science.


00:46:56.760 --> 00:46:58.637
It's the same as most
engineers around here.


00:46:58.800 --> 00:47:01.314
We now require advanced
extension courses


00:47:01.400 --> 00:47:02.913
through the University
of Virginia.


00:47:03.080 --> 00:47:05.275
It's in the employee
handbook. In addendum.


00:47:06.560 --> 00:47:07.680
In case you haven't read it.


00:47:08.320 --> 00:47:10.470
Every time we have a chance
to get ahead...


00:47:10.680 --> 00:47:11.829
they move the finish line.


00:47:12.000 --> 00:47:13.400
I just follow the rules
around here.


00:47:13.600 --> 00:47:15.113
And I expect everyone
who works for me


00:47:15.240 --> 00:47:16.240
to follow them as well.


00:47:16.400 --> 00:47:19.597
There are no special
circumstances for anyone.


00:47:20.080 --> 00:47:22.230
Y'all should be thankful
you have jobs at all.


00:47:28.080 --> 00:47:31.550
"For those that cannot attend classes
at the University of Virginia...


00:47:31.720 --> 00:47:34.109
"advanced extension courses
are available


00:47:34.280 --> 00:47:36.077
"at the Hampton High School."


00:47:36.800 --> 00:47:39.075
That's still a
segregated school, Mary.


00:47:39.240 --> 00:47:41.708
Virginia acts like Brown
versus Board of Education


00:47:41.800 --> 00:47:42.800
never happened.


00:47:43.000 --> 00:47:45.275
They are never gonna
allow a colored woman


00:47:45.440 --> 00:47:47.431
to just take classes
at an all-white school.


00:47:47.600 --> 00:47:49.397
Well, sounds about right.


00:47:49.560 --> 00:47:51.278
That's all you gonna say?


00:47:51.360 --> 00:47:53.096
I'm not gonna sit here all day
and listen to you complain


00:47:53.120 --> 00:47:54.314
about the way things are.


00:47:54.480 --> 00:47:55.560
We're trying to play cards.


00:47:55.680 --> 00:47:57.296
Petition the court.
Fight for what you want.


00:47:57.320 --> 00:47:58.799
But quit talking about it.


00:48:01.800 --> 00:48:03.438
I've been complaining?
For days.


00:48:03.600 --> 00:48:04.600
Ladies.


00:48:05.800 --> 00:48:07.916
Not one more peep
about work.


00:48:08.080 --> 00:48:09.149
Deal me in.


00:48:09.280 --> 00:48:10.280
Katherine?


00:48:10.320 --> 00:48:11.719
- Come on.
- Oh, no.


00:48:11.840 --> 00:48:12.909
Right here.
You.


00:48:13.080 --> 00:48:14.957
Child, I needed this.


00:48:15.160 --> 00:48:16.160
Thank you.


00:48:18.640 --> 00:48:20.835
Don't give her
anymore, Katherine.


00:48:21.040 --> 00:48:23.156
I can't. She drank it all.


00:48:23.320 --> 00:48:25.595
Listen, I'm fine.


00:48:25.760 --> 00:48:27.637
I'm as fine
as I have ever been.


00:48:27.840 --> 00:48:29.592
You're drunk
as you've ever been.


00:48:31.000 --> 00:48:32.513
Come on. Let's dance, ladies.


00:48:34.160 --> 00:48:35.434
Come on, Kat. Let's dance.


00:48:35.640 --> 00:48:36.920
Go and dance with her,
Katherine.


00:48:37.040 --> 00:48:38.393
Come on.
Get out here.


00:48:38.480 --> 00:48:39.595
Hide that.


00:48:39.680 --> 00:48:40.776
We gonna teach you
how to dance.


00:48:40.800 --> 00:48:41.800
You go ahead.


00:48:41.880 --> 00:48:43.836
All right.
It's too low.


00:48:44.000 --> 00:48:45.638
We gonna teach you
how to get a man.


00:48:45.720 --> 00:48:47.631
- Teach me? What?
- That's right. Come on!


00:48:47.720 --> 00:48:49.358
You got to know
how to dance.


00:48:49.520 --> 00:48:50.999
Slow. Slow.
Come on.


00:48:51.160 --> 00:48:53.116
Twirl it around.
Twirl, twirl.


00:48:53.320 --> 00:48:55.436
Yes!
Twirl, twirl, twirl.


00:48:55.520 --> 00:48:57.192
Whoo! Look at her,
Dorothy.


00:48:57.360 --> 00:48:59.351
Can I win me one of those
fancy dance contests?


00:48:59.520 --> 00:49:00.794
Whip it around.


00:49:02.800 --> 00:49:04.376
Yeah, and on the outside.


00:49:04.400 --> 00:49:05.674
Was it better than Mama's?


00:49:05.880 --> 00:49:07.154
No, not really.


00:49:07.320 --> 00:49:08.840
Segregation must go!


00:49:09.240 --> 00:49:12.710
Segregation must go!


00:49:12.880 --> 00:49:14.916
Segregation must go!


00:49:15.080 --> 00:49:16.877
Segregation must go!


00:49:21.160 --> 00:49:22.376
Don't pay attention
to all that.


00:49:22.400 --> 00:49:23.880
We're not part
of that trouble. Go on.


00:49:35.680 --> 00:49:37.875
"Mind. They must have done..."


00:49:42.240 --> 00:49:44.549
We don't want any trouble
in here.


00:49:45.240 --> 00:49:47.356
Oh, I'm not here
for any trouble, ma'am.


00:49:47.520 --> 00:49:48.589
What are you here for?


00:49:49.400 --> 00:49:50.400
A book.


00:49:50.560 --> 00:49:52.915
You have books
in the colored section.


00:49:54.240 --> 00:49:55.912
It doesn't have
what I'm looking for.


00:49:56.080 --> 00:49:58.230
That's just
the way it is.


00:50:02.400 --> 00:50:03.736
Go on, now.
You know better than this.


00:50:03.760 --> 00:50:06.069
Get your hands off my boys!
Don't touch them.


00:50:07.280 --> 00:50:09.032
You have a blessed day.


00:50:17.560 --> 00:50:20.916
Separate and equal
are two different things.


00:50:21.120 --> 00:50:23.634
Just 'cause it's the way,
doesn't make it right.


00:50:23.800 --> 00:50:24.915
Understand?


00:50:25.960 --> 00:50:26.960
Yes, Mama.


00:50:27.000 --> 00:50:29.275
You act right, you are right.


00:50:29.440 --> 00:50:31.078
That's for certain.


00:50:31.240 --> 00:50:32.240
Understand?


00:50:32.400 --> 00:50:33.435
Yes, Mama.


00:50:39.640 --> 00:50:41.312
You took that book, Mama?


00:50:41.480 --> 00:50:43.436
Son, I pay taxes.


00:50:43.640 --> 00:50:45.631
And taxes pay for everything
in that library.


00:50:45.800 --> 00:50:47.791
Can't take something
you already paid for.


00:50:52.480 --> 00:50:55.950
"Fortran is a new
and exciting language...


00:50:56.120 --> 00:50:59.476
"used by programmers
to communicate with computers.


00:50:59.640 --> 00:51:02.950
"It is exciting, as it is
the wave of the future."


00:51:03.960 --> 00:51:05.439
All right, everybody smile.


00:51:05.520 --> 00:51:07.080
- Everybody smile.
- Aw!


00:51:10.480 --> 00:51:11.480
Make a wish.
Make a wish.


00:51:11.640 --> 00:51:13.119
Blow. Try again.


00:51:13.280 --> 00:51:14.793
There you go.


00:51:16.040 --> 00:51:17.951
Leonard, you need
to get you a job now.


00:51:18.080 --> 00:51:19.080
Two jobs.


00:51:20.360 --> 00:51:21.856
Your baby is nine.
What are you gonna do?


00:51:21.880 --> 00:51:23.496
Thank you, man.
Yeah, you're just in time.


00:51:23.520 --> 00:51:24.839
Look at that.


00:51:25.000 --> 00:51:26.991
Look at what?
What a surprise.


00:51:27.160 --> 00:51:28.673
Can't complain, man.


00:51:29.720 --> 00:51:31.472
I'll say.


00:51:31.680 --> 00:51:33.671
Howard must have invited him.
Mmm-hmm.


00:51:34.200 --> 00:51:36.555
Let me go help them
with those flowers.


00:51:46.240 --> 00:51:48.151
Walter, you behave yourself.


00:51:48.320 --> 00:51:50.788
Now, you know,
I'm just... Yeah.


00:51:52.680 --> 00:51:54.398
That's right, baby.
Shake it.


00:51:55.360 --> 00:51:57.191
Yeah. There you go.


00:52:00.840 --> 00:52:03.195
Good to see you.
Yes. Thank you.


00:52:11.760 --> 00:52:13.876
I believe
I owe you an apology.


00:52:16.200 --> 00:52:18.509
Well, was that it?


00:52:19.240 --> 00:52:20.992
Can I make it
while we dance?


00:52:33.040 --> 00:52:35.600
I'm afraid I simply misspoke
the other day, Mrs. Goble.


00:52:37.880 --> 00:52:40.110
I've been away
for a long time.


00:52:41.040 --> 00:52:43.873
I imagine I'm just
out of practice.


00:52:44.560 --> 00:52:45.754
Mmm-hmm.


00:52:46.360 --> 00:52:48.794
I was hoping you'd allow
me to start over.


00:52:50.240 --> 00:52:51.240
Mmm-hmm.


00:52:52.040 --> 00:52:53.598
See, the thing is...


00:52:53.760 --> 00:52:55.680
I have an interest in
getting to know you better.


00:52:56.280 --> 00:52:57.280
Mmm-hmm.


00:52:59.280 --> 00:53:00.920
You're one
tough customer, huh?


00:53:03.440 --> 00:53:05.158
You haven't said
you're sorry yet.


00:53:05.320 --> 00:53:06.548
Right.


00:53:06.720 --> 00:53:07.755
Of course.


00:53:11.880 --> 00:53:13.757
I'm sorry, Katherine.


00:53:13.960 --> 00:53:15.109
For what, Jim?


00:53:15.920 --> 00:53:17.717
Underestimating you.


00:53:18.600 --> 00:53:20.397
And any other woman
like you...


00:53:22.560 --> 00:53:24.755
Though I don't imagine
there's many.


00:53:26.800 --> 00:53:28.950
That's good practice,
right there.


00:53:32.120 --> 00:53:33.736
We interrupt
this musical programming


00:53:33.760 --> 00:53:35.478
with breaking news.


00:53:35.640 --> 00:53:37.790
The Soviet news agency
has announced


00:53:37.960 --> 00:53:39.996
that Russian cosmonaut,
Yuri Gagarin,


00:53:40.120 --> 00:53:42.315
has become
the first man in space.


00:53:42.480 --> 00:53:43.480
Gagarin completed


00:53:43.600 --> 00:53:44.840
one full orbit
around the planet


00:53:45.000 --> 00:53:47.150
in 108 minutes.


00:53:47.320 --> 00:53:49.000
Stay tuned to this station
for more details


00:53:49.160 --> 00:53:50.479
as they become available.


00:53:51.800 --> 00:53:53.995
Guess that puts us
behind the curve.


00:53:54.160 --> 00:53:55.480
The Vostok-1 rocket...


00:53:55.520 --> 00:53:58.796
delivered Russian cosmonaut
Yuri Gagarin's 3KA capsule...


00:53:58.960 --> 00:54:00.837
into orbit, at 6:07 UTC...


00:54:01.640 --> 00:54:05.349
Reentering Russian airspace
at 7: 55 UTC.


00:54:05.520 --> 00:54:07.078
Proving conclusively that man


00:54:07.160 --> 00:54:08.798
could survive
orbital space flight.


00:54:09.200 --> 00:54:10.519
Gagarin was awarded


00:54:10.680 --> 00:54:12.016
the Order of Lenin
and a Gold Star...


00:54:12.040 --> 00:54:13.837
Well, they beat
the pants off us.


00:54:14.000 --> 00:54:15.115
Let's go.


00:54:15.280 --> 00:54:16.336
He is also conferred
the title...


00:54:16.360 --> 00:54:17.600
Let's learn how to fly
to space.


00:54:17.680 --> 00:54:19.875
"First Soviet Astronaut"...


00:54:20.040 --> 00:54:22.838
and has been proclaimed
a national hero.


00:54:23.000 --> 00:54:25.958
This is a day
of unprecedented triumph...


00:54:26.120 --> 00:54:27.872
for the Russian
space program


00:54:28.040 --> 00:54:30.270
and a crushing blow
for America


00:54:30.360 --> 00:54:32.351
in the race for space.


00:54:32.520 --> 00:54:34.351
I wanna thank everyone
for staying.


00:54:35.520 --> 00:54:37.670
I know it's late.
And after what I just saw,


00:54:37.840 --> 00:54:40.479
I'm sure everyone's probably
anxious to just go home.


00:54:40.920 --> 00:54:43.229
Before we do, I just
have to ask a question.


00:54:44.840 --> 00:54:46.717
The same one


00:54:46.840 --> 00:54:48.656
I've been asking myself
all the way over here...


00:54:48.680 --> 00:54:49.715
which is...


00:54:51.600 --> 00:54:53.079
How the hell did
we find ourself


00:54:53.200 --> 00:54:55.077
in second place
in a two-man race?


00:54:55.880 --> 00:54:56.880
Anybody?


00:54:57.880 --> 00:54:58.880
Paul?


00:55:00.400 --> 00:55:01.880
And I think Glenn said it
pretty well.


00:55:02.040 --> 00:55:03.120
They're just outworking us.


00:55:03.200 --> 00:55:05.430
The reason
I have to think that


00:55:05.600 --> 00:55:06.696
is because I refuse
to believe


00:55:06.720 --> 00:55:08.278
that the Russians
are smarter than us


00:55:08.440 --> 00:55:09.953
or that they have
more technology,


00:55:10.040 --> 00:55:11.553
or that they care more.


00:55:13.280 --> 00:55:15.111
But I don't know.
Is that possible?


00:55:16.360 --> 00:55:17.634
Is it possible
that it actually


00:55:17.720 --> 00:55:19.233
means more to them?


00:55:20.760 --> 00:55:23.911
We're in the fight
of our lives, people.


00:55:24.080 --> 00:55:26.389
We're not on
some epic joy ride.


00:55:26.560 --> 00:55:28.232
I just saw seven faces


00:55:28.560 --> 00:55:30.391
who aren't sure
that we can get there.


00:55:34.920 --> 00:55:36.717
This is our problem.


00:55:37.560 --> 00:55:39.073
And so as of right now


00:55:41.040 --> 00:55:43.056
there's only two things you
need to know going forward.


00:55:43.080 --> 00:55:44.593
One is staying here,
working late,


00:55:44.720 --> 00:55:45.920
that's gonna be
a fact of life.


00:55:47.120 --> 00:55:48.599
And two, don't expect
your paychecks


00:55:48.720 --> 00:55:50.256
to reflect the extra time
it's gonna take


00:55:50.280 --> 00:55:52.236
to catch up
and pass those bastards.


00:55:54.160 --> 00:55:55.736
For those of you
who can't work that way,


00:55:55.760 --> 00:55:58.115
I understand and thank you
for what you've done.


00:55:59.280 --> 00:56:00.759
For everyone else...


00:56:01.720 --> 00:56:03.995
I suggest
you call your wives


00:56:04.160 --> 00:56:05.559
and tell them
how it's gonna be.


00:56:06.000 --> 00:56:07.479
I'll start with mine.


00:57:04.840 --> 00:57:07.035
Sam, turn it up.
Turn it up.


00:57:07.200 --> 00:57:08.200
Yes, sir.


00:57:10.480 --> 00:57:12.038
John.
Morning, sir.


00:57:17.040 --> 00:57:20.032
7-5, 7-4, 7-3...


00:57:27.240 --> 00:57:28.673
End of the day.


00:57:34.680 --> 00:57:35.874
Okay, we're looking good.


00:57:40.720 --> 00:57:43.029
Okay.


00:57:52.760 --> 00:57:54.159
Sir, might be
the selector latch.


00:57:54.280 --> 00:57:55.508
I'm taking a walk.


00:57:55.680 --> 00:57:57.280
We're honored
to serve our country.


00:57:57.440 --> 00:57:59.670
So we don't think
about danger.


00:57:59.840 --> 00:58:01.831
We don't have time
to be scared.


00:58:02.040 --> 00:58:03.871
We got a job to do.


00:58:09.920 --> 00:58:12.070
Good morning.
Good morning.


00:58:12.240 --> 00:58:14.356
Mrs. Jackson.


00:58:14.520 --> 00:58:16.715
Colored seats are at the back
of the court room.


00:58:16.880 --> 00:58:18.233
Thank you, ma'am.


00:58:37.400 --> 00:58:39.277
So, which one of you
wants to go first?


00:59:02.800 --> 00:59:07.271
The IBM 7090
data processing system.


00:59:07.480 --> 00:59:09.630
It has the capability
of solving


00:59:09.800 --> 00:59:12.792
over 24,000 multiplications


00:59:14.120 --> 00:59:15.120
per second.


00:59:15.320 --> 00:59:16.992
Holy Moses,
that's lightning fast.


00:59:17.160 --> 00:59:18.360
They'll never
get that to work.


00:59:18.640 --> 00:59:20.756
Oh, it'll run eventually.


00:59:20.920 --> 00:59:22.960
And when it does, we have
to know how to program it.


00:59:23.160 --> 00:59:24.560
Unless you'd rather be
out of a job.


00:59:24.640 --> 00:59:26.119
No, ma'am.


00:59:31.880 --> 00:59:33.279
Mr. Z...


00:59:34.040 --> 00:59:35.792
I got a court date.


00:59:39.840 --> 00:59:41.159
"The computer uses it


00:59:41.280 --> 00:59:42.838
"as its high speed
storage unit,


00:59:43.000 --> 00:59:45.036
"an information
holding device."


00:59:45.160 --> 00:59:46.160
Hmm.


00:59:46.960 --> 00:59:48.154
You have a brain.


00:59:48.320 --> 00:59:49.833
That I can work with.


00:59:53.520 --> 00:59:55.511
This is in the wrong spot.


01:00:00.360 --> 01:00:02.191
Attagirl.


01:00:04.480 --> 01:00:05.480
Ruth.


01:00:05.800 --> 01:00:07.256
Yes, sir. Get me the
Cape on the line.


01:00:07.280 --> 01:00:09.111
Shepard's trajectories
need to be updated.


01:00:12.000 --> 01:00:13.035
Wow.


01:00:14.880 --> 01:00:15.949
Where is she?


01:00:20.080 --> 01:00:22.120
You and I are no
different from each other


01:00:23.520 --> 01:00:25.272
Shut our eyes
when we slumber I see numbers


01:00:26.920 --> 01:00:30.071
Black and white
we're computers, I am color


01:00:30.680 --> 01:00:32.440
Don't act like
you was there when you wasn't


01:00:38.360 --> 01:00:40.999
I don't want no free ride


01:00:45.680 --> 01:00:48.319
Some nights I cry


01:00:48.400 --> 01:00:50.436
'Cause I can
see the day comin'


01:00:52.600 --> 01:00:54.591
Together we fly


01:00:55.480 --> 01:00:57.994
Oh, but no more runnin'


01:01:02.920 --> 01:01:04.558
No more runnin'


01:01:14.560 --> 01:01:15.680
Where the hell
have you been?


01:01:16.120 --> 01:01:18.315
Everywhere I look, you're not
where I need you to be.


01:01:18.480 --> 01:01:19.629
It's not my imagination.


01:01:19.800 --> 01:01:21.916
Now where the hell
do you go every day?


01:01:24.800 --> 01:01:26.392
To the bathroom, sir.


01:01:26.560 --> 01:01:27.754
To the bathroom?


01:01:28.400 --> 01:01:29.549
To the damn bathroom.


01:01:30.880 --> 01:01:32.108
For 40 minutes a day?


01:01:35.160 --> 01:01:36.673
What do you do
in there?


01:01:37.760 --> 01:01:40.320
We're T-minus zero here.
I put a lot of faith in you.


01:01:42.520 --> 01:01:44.909
There's no bathroom
for me here.


01:01:45.760 --> 01:01:47.496
What do you mean there is
no bathroom for you here?


01:01:47.520 --> 01:01:49.875
There is no bathroom.


01:01:50.040 --> 01:01:52.759
There are no colored
bathrooms in this building


01:01:52.920 --> 01:01:54.876
or any building
outside the West Campus,


01:01:55.000 --> 01:01:56.319
which is half a mile away.


01:01:56.520 --> 01:01:57.520
Did you know that?


01:01:59.200 --> 01:02:02.636
I have to walk to Timbuktu
just to relieve myself.


01:02:02.800 --> 01:02:04.870
And I can't use
one of the handy bikes.


01:02:05.040 --> 01:02:06.951
Picture that, Mr. Harrison.


01:02:07.720 --> 01:02:08.789
My uniform...


01:02:08.960 --> 01:02:11.679
Skirt below my knees,
my heels,


01:02:11.760 --> 01:02:13.159
and a simple string of pearls.


01:02:13.320 --> 01:02:14.992
Well, I don't own pearls.


01:02:15.160 --> 01:02:16.957
Lord knows you don't pay
coloreds enough


01:02:17.040 --> 01:02:18.553
to afford pearls!


01:02:19.480 --> 01:02:23.553
And I work like a dog,
day and night,


01:02:23.760 --> 01:02:25.830
living off of coffee
from a pot


01:02:25.920 --> 01:02:27.399
none of you wanna touch!


01:02:40.160 --> 01:02:42.720
So, excuse me


01:02:42.880 --> 01:02:44.472
if I have to go
to the restroom


01:02:44.560 --> 01:02:46.312
a few times a day.


01:03:57.760 --> 01:03:59.159
There you have it.


01:04:03.560 --> 01:04:04.913
No more colored restrooms.


01:04:06.000 --> 01:04:08.230
No more white restrooms.


01:04:08.400 --> 01:04:09.992
Just plain old toilets.


01:04:11.280 --> 01:04:12.713
Go wherever
you damn well please.


01:04:13.280 --> 01:04:14.918
Preferably closer
to your desk.


01:04:22.640 --> 01:04:26.030
Here at NASA,
we all pee the same color.


01:04:52.600 --> 01:04:54.238
Heard you was
fighting the flu.


01:04:54.400 --> 01:04:56.118
Figured you could
use some soup.


01:04:56.280 --> 01:04:57.713
Hot meal for the girls.


01:04:58.720 --> 01:05:00.517
Come on in.


01:05:06.640 --> 01:05:07.959
Excuse me.


01:05:08.120 --> 01:05:10.554
Okay, ladies.
Let's say grace.


01:05:11.600 --> 01:05:13.113
Bow your heads.


01:05:17.000 --> 01:05:19.560
Dear Lord, thank you
for this meal.


01:05:19.720 --> 01:05:21.756
We ask that you
bless this food


01:05:21.840 --> 01:05:23.876
so that it may
bless our bodies.


01:05:24.040 --> 01:05:25.996
In Jesus's name we pray.


01:05:27.920 --> 01:05:29.558
Amen.
Amen.


01:05:33.000 --> 01:05:35.912
It's a historic day,
here at Cape Canaveral.


01:05:36.080 --> 01:05:37.399
And here's what we can expect.


01:05:37.560 --> 01:05:40.438
Alan Shepard and Freedom 7
will be launched into space


01:05:40.600 --> 01:05:43.273
at an altitude of about
116 miles an hour.


01:05:43.360 --> 01:05:45.316
Dorothy, come on, you gonna miss it!
Oh, boy! Go.


01:05:45.400 --> 01:05:46.656
- Come on, Dorothy.
- I'm coming.


01:05:46.680 --> 01:05:48.477
...then he will land
around 35 miles


01:05:48.560 --> 01:05:49.640
off the coast of Florida...


01:05:49.840 --> 01:05:50.955
where he'll be picked up


01:05:51.120 --> 01:05:52.496
by the men
of the USS Lake Champlain...


01:05:52.520 --> 01:05:53.520
Finally. Come over here.


01:05:53.720 --> 01:05:55.551
who set out
for this historic purpose.


01:05:57.000 --> 01:05:58.616
Freedom 7,
we're going to have to bleed off


01:05:58.640 --> 01:06:01.359
some of the pressure
inside the liquid oxygen tank.


01:06:01.440 --> 01:06:04.512
That should reduce the temperature
inside the capsule. Over.


01:06:04.680 --> 01:06:06.238
Any day now, CAPCOM.


01:06:06.440 --> 01:06:08.240
Fix your little problem
and light this candle.


01:06:08.640 --> 01:06:09.959
Roger that, Freedom 7.


01:06:10.120 --> 01:06:11.519
Stand by.


01:06:11.680 --> 01:06:12.896
Temperature is returning
to normal.


01:06:12.920 --> 01:06:14.876
Running one more
launch status check.


01:06:15.040 --> 01:06:16.109
All systems now go.


01:06:16.320 --> 01:06:17.799
Move to final countdown
sequence.


01:06:17.960 --> 01:06:19.473
Moving to final
countdown sequence.


01:06:19.560 --> 01:06:20.560
Roger that.


01:06:21.400 --> 01:06:22.799
We're at T-minus 10...


01:06:23.480 --> 01:06:24.549
nine...


01:06:24.720 --> 01:06:25.835
eight...


01:06:26.040 --> 01:06:27.314
seven...


01:06:27.480 --> 01:06:28.674
six...


01:06:28.880 --> 01:06:30.074
five...


01:06:30.240 --> 01:06:31.514
four...


01:06:31.680 --> 01:06:32.680
three...


01:06:32.800 --> 01:06:33.994
two...


01:06:34.160 --> 01:06:35.434
one...


01:06:35.600 --> 01:06:36.828
zero.


01:06:39.120 --> 01:06:40.348
We have ignition.


01:06:41.040 --> 01:06:42.314
And we have lift off.


01:06:43.280 --> 01:06:44.508
Get up there.


01:06:44.680 --> 01:06:46.910
Lift off,
and the clock is started.


01:06:50.720 --> 01:06:53.632
Fuel is go. 2.5G.


01:06:53.800 --> 01:06:54.869
Get on up there.


01:06:55.040 --> 01:06:56.234
- Come on!
- Lift off at


01:06:56.320 --> 01:06:58.197
34 minutes after the hour.


01:07:18.800 --> 01:07:20.995
Pitch 88.
Trajectory is good.


01:07:23.880 --> 01:07:25.552
Yaw is okay.


01:07:25.720 --> 01:07:27.517
Switching manual roll.


01:07:27.680 --> 01:07:29.671
Yes!


01:07:31.680 --> 01:07:33.636
For the eyes of the world


01:07:33.800 --> 01:07:35.916
now look into space,


01:07:36.080 --> 01:07:38.389
to the Moon
and to the planets beyond.


01:07:38.480 --> 01:07:39.480
Yes!
We did it!


01:07:39.560 --> 01:07:41.676
And we have vowed...


01:07:41.840 --> 01:07:44.195
that we shall not
see it governed...


01:07:44.360 --> 01:07:46.590
by a hostile flag
of conquest...


01:07:47.280 --> 01:07:50.511
but by a banner
of freedom and peace.


01:07:51.240 --> 01:07:53.879
We set sail on this new sea...


01:07:54.040 --> 01:07:57.316
because there is new knowledge
to be gained...


01:07:57.480 --> 01:07:59.357
and new rights to be won.


01:07:59.840 --> 01:08:02.434
We choose to go to the Moon
in this decade


01:08:02.640 --> 01:08:04.198
and do the other things...


01:08:04.360 --> 01:08:06.032
not because they are easy...


01:08:06.200 --> 01:08:07.997
but because they are hard.


01:08:08.840 --> 01:08:10.637
Suppose we should all
thank President Kennedy


01:08:10.800 --> 01:08:12.677
for the continued
job security.


01:08:16.640 --> 01:08:18.153
Uh, Gus Grissom's up next.


01:08:18.600 --> 01:08:20.830
Another suborbital flight
with a Redstone.


01:08:21.680 --> 01:08:22.749
It's our last shot


01:08:22.840 --> 01:08:24.239
before we strap Glenn
to the Atlas


01:08:25.360 --> 01:08:26.839
and pray we can bring him
back down.


01:08:27.040 --> 01:08:28.519
Yes, and I imagine
he'd like us


01:08:28.600 --> 01:08:30.352
to have the math
worked out beforehand.


01:08:32.200 --> 01:08:33.952
Good work, gentlemen.


01:08:34.120 --> 01:08:36.236
Go have dinner
with your kids


01:08:36.400 --> 01:08:37.958
and let's get
back to work.


01:08:50.200 --> 01:08:54.113
Look what you've done to me


01:08:54.560 --> 01:08:59.031
Make me behave like
I wouldn't ever


01:08:59.400 --> 01:09:01.391
I know you're the one for me


01:09:03.280 --> 01:09:07.114
Your essence is the ink
in the word "forever"


01:09:10.600 --> 01:09:13.068
I think it's high time that
I kissed you, Katherine.


01:09:14.000 --> 01:09:16.434
Oh, and why is that?


01:09:18.560 --> 01:09:21.552
Figured you attracted to me,
same as I to you.


01:09:23.000 --> 01:09:24.831
Is that right?
Mmm-hmm.


01:09:25.640 --> 01:09:28.108
Or you would have told me to
get going a long time ago.


01:09:42.600 --> 01:09:45.114
Haven't kissed a man
in a long time.


01:09:46.200 --> 01:09:47.599
Almost forgot how to.


01:10:01.080 --> 01:10:03.548
The FBI
has arrested four white men


01:10:03.640 --> 01:10:05.073
accused of throwing
a fire bomb


01:10:05.240 --> 01:10:06.593
into a Freedom Rider bus


01:10:06.720 --> 01:10:08.790
outside of Anniston,
Alabama...


01:10:08.960 --> 01:10:10.480
- Hi, babies.
- The bus filled rapidly


01:10:10.600 --> 01:10:11.776
Hi, Mama.
...with black smoke.


01:10:11.800 --> 01:10:13.028
We all hit the floor.


01:10:13.200 --> 01:10:15.430
All of us realized
the bus was on fire...


01:10:15.600 --> 01:10:17.670
and had to go out
into the mob.


01:10:17.840 --> 01:10:19.056
The kids don't need
to be watching this.


01:10:19.080 --> 01:10:20.399
They need to see this.


01:10:20.560 --> 01:10:22.120
When we got off the bus
in Birmingham...


01:10:22.280 --> 01:10:23.633
Everybody needs to see this.


01:10:23.800 --> 01:10:25.552
...took me and forced me
out of the door...


01:10:25.720 --> 01:10:26.869
and threw me into the crowd.


01:10:27.040 --> 01:10:29.634
And then I was beaten by one
big guy and several others.


01:10:29.800 --> 01:10:32.951
We think we're rendering
a great service to our nation.


01:10:33.160 --> 01:10:35.833
For this is not a struggle
for ourselves alone.


01:10:36.040 --> 01:10:38.873
It is a struggle
to save the soul of America.


01:10:39.040 --> 01:10:40.155
Mary Jackson.


01:10:40.720 --> 01:10:43.837
Petition to attend courses
at Hampton High School.


01:10:47.280 --> 01:10:48.679
Good morning, Your Honor.


01:10:53.280 --> 01:10:55.874
Hampton High School is
a white school, Mrs. Jackson.


01:10:57.120 --> 01:10:59.270
Yes, Your Honor.
I'm aware of that.


01:10:59.480 --> 01:11:01.152
Virginia,
still a segregated state.


01:11:01.360 --> 01:11:03.237
Regardless of what
the federal government says,


01:11:03.440 --> 01:11:05.590
regardless of what
the Supreme Court says,


01:11:05.760 --> 01:11:07.557
our law is the law.


01:11:08.360 --> 01:11:10.555
Your Honor, if I may.


01:11:10.720 --> 01:11:12.392
I believe there are
special circumstances


01:11:12.480 --> 01:11:13.480
to be considered.


01:11:13.640 --> 01:11:15.278
What would warrant
a colored woman


01:11:15.360 --> 01:11:16.360
attending a white school?


01:11:18.280 --> 01:11:20.032
May I approach
your bench, sir?


01:11:33.160 --> 01:11:34.160
Your Honor...


01:11:34.680 --> 01:11:36.398
you of all people
should understand


01:11:36.520 --> 01:11:37.953
the importance of being first.


01:11:38.760 --> 01:11:40.398
How's that, Mrs. Jackson?


01:11:41.120 --> 01:11:43.190
You were the first
in your family


01:11:43.280 --> 01:11:45.157
to serve in the Armed Forces.


01:11:45.360 --> 01:11:46.509
U.S. Navy.


01:11:46.680 --> 01:11:48.989
The first
to attend university.


01:11:49.200 --> 01:11:50.679
George Mason.


01:11:50.840 --> 01:11:53.035
And the first state judge
to be recommissioned


01:11:53.240 --> 01:11:55.549
by three consecutive
governors.


01:11:55.720 --> 01:11:57.392
You've done some research.


01:11:57.840 --> 01:11:59.159
Yes, sir.


01:11:59.600 --> 01:12:00.919
What's the point?


01:12:01.080 --> 01:12:02.399
The point is, Your Honor...


01:12:02.560 --> 01:12:04.755
no Negro woman
in the state of Virginia...


01:12:04.960 --> 01:12:07.269
has ever attended
an all-white high school.


01:12:07.440 --> 01:12:08.440
It's unheard of.


01:12:08.600 --> 01:12:10.192
Yeah. Unheard of.


01:12:11.000 --> 01:12:13.560
And before Alan Shepard
sat on top of a rocket,


01:12:13.760 --> 01:12:15.830
no other American
had ever touched space.


01:12:16.360 --> 01:12:18.316
And now, he will forever
be remembered


01:12:18.520 --> 01:12:20.909
as the U.S. Navy man
from New Hampshire,


01:12:21.080 --> 01:12:23.116
the first to touch the stars.


01:12:24.840 --> 01:12:25.989
And I, sir...


01:12:26.160 --> 01:12:28.674
I plan on being
an engineer at NASA,


01:12:28.840 --> 01:12:30.751
but I can't do that
without taking them classes


01:12:30.920 --> 01:12:32.592
at that all-white high school.


01:12:33.520 --> 01:12:36.080
And I can't change
the color of my skin.


01:12:36.880 --> 01:12:40.395
So I have no choice
but to be the first.


01:12:41.320 --> 01:12:43.151
Which I can't do
without you, sir.


01:12:44.400 --> 01:12:45.400
Your Honor...


01:12:45.440 --> 01:12:47.954
out of all the cases
you're gonna hear today...


01:12:48.120 --> 01:12:50.634
which one is gonna matter
100 years from now?


01:12:51.480 --> 01:12:54.233
Which one is gonna
make you the first?


01:12:57.200 --> 01:12:58.200
Hmm.


01:13:05.400 --> 01:13:07.072
Only the night classes...


01:13:08.080 --> 01:13:09.513
Mrs. Jackson.


01:13:16.560 --> 01:13:19.074
Yes, yes, yes!


01:13:19.240 --> 01:13:20.309
Yes!


01:13:21.600 --> 01:13:24.068
Oh, my God.
I cannot believe.


01:13:48.600 --> 01:13:49.656
How late did he say
he was gonna be?


01:13:49.680 --> 01:13:51.576
Excuse me.
No more than five minutes, sir.


01:13:51.600 --> 01:13:53.079
Gus Grissom's
coordinates.


01:13:53.280 --> 01:13:56.192
Uh, I'd like to get a jump
on John Glenn's trajectory.


01:13:57.640 --> 01:13:59.200
Do you have any idea
what you're asking?


01:13:59.240 --> 01:14:02.437
An orbital launch with an Atlas
rocket is going to take time.


01:14:02.640 --> 01:14:04.392
Is there a problem?


01:14:05.640 --> 01:14:06.914
Mr. Harrison...


01:14:07.760 --> 01:14:09.318
I was wondering.


01:14:09.480 --> 01:14:11.277
Uh-huh. I've been
wondering my whole life,


01:14:11.360 --> 01:14:12.998
so you just
spit it out.


01:14:13.160 --> 01:14:15.071
Well, sir...


01:14:17.160 --> 01:14:20.550
I would like to get a jump
on John Glenn's trajectory.


01:14:20.720 --> 01:14:21.776
This isn't about
plugging in numbers,


01:14:21.800 --> 01:14:23.000
it's about inventing the math.


01:14:23.160 --> 01:14:24.856
And I think you and I
talked about that, right?


01:14:24.880 --> 01:14:25.880
It doesn't exist.


01:14:26.040 --> 01:14:27.040
I can do it, sir.


01:14:27.200 --> 01:14:28.633
Do you have any idea
how exacting


01:14:28.720 --> 01:14:30.233
these calculations have to be?


01:14:30.440 --> 01:14:32.829
It's like shooting a sawed-off
shotgun from a thousand feet


01:14:32.960 --> 01:14:35.696
and getting that one BB through a
hole so tiny, you can't even see it.


01:14:35.720 --> 01:14:36.736
Okay, let me see
what you've got.


01:14:36.760 --> 01:14:37.840
I'm an excellent shot, sir.


01:14:38.440 --> 01:14:40.476
How have the
Grissom numbers been?


01:14:41.080 --> 01:14:42.559
They've been spot on.


01:14:45.240 --> 01:14:46.468
Give Glenn's orbit a go.


01:14:48.040 --> 01:14:49.696
But you run it all through
Stafford, you understand?


01:14:49.720 --> 01:14:50.720
Yes, sir.


01:14:51.280 --> 01:14:52.349
Thank you.


01:14:56.560 --> 01:14:58.278
Computers don't
author reports.


01:15:05.240 --> 01:15:07.674
CAPCOM,
this is Liberty Bell 7.


01:15:07.760 --> 01:15:08.760
Loud and clear.


01:15:10.960 --> 01:15:12.837
Okay, it's a nice
ride up to now.


01:15:13.000 --> 01:15:14.000
Roger.


01:15:14.600 --> 01:15:16.716
Liberty Bell 7,
this is Hook 1 approaching.


01:15:16.800 --> 01:15:17.800
Ready to hook on. Over.


01:15:17.960 --> 01:15:20.190
I read you loud
and clear. Over.


01:15:21.120 --> 01:15:22.360
The hatch is blown,
CAPCOM.


01:15:22.400 --> 01:15:25.597
The Liberty Bell 7
capsule is taking on water.


01:15:25.760 --> 01:15:27.671
Gus Grissom
is out of the capsule.


01:15:27.840 --> 01:15:30.354
Rescuers are now going
to pull him


01:15:30.440 --> 01:15:31.440
from the ocean waves.


01:15:31.600 --> 01:15:35.673
Unfortunately, the Liberty
Bell 7 capsule is lost.


01:15:35.880 --> 01:15:36.880
Mama.


01:15:38.800 --> 01:15:39.800
I can't, I can't.
Mary.


01:15:47.080 --> 01:15:48.136
Mr. Harrison,


01:15:48.160 --> 01:15:49.480
taxpayer money
sank into the ocean


01:15:49.520 --> 01:15:50.794
along with your capsule.


01:15:50.960 --> 01:15:51.960
So what makes you think


01:15:52.040 --> 01:15:53.837
you can bring
John Glenn home safely?


01:15:54.000 --> 01:15:55.000
Let me say first...


01:15:55.080 --> 01:15:56.480
discovery is never
just for the sake


01:15:56.520 --> 01:15:57.560
of discovery, gentlemen...


01:15:57.680 --> 01:15:59.750
but for the sake
of human survival.


01:16:00.360 --> 01:16:02.635
And it will always
come with a risk.


01:16:04.240 --> 01:16:06.310
Whoever gets there first
will make the rules.


01:16:06.480 --> 01:16:08.471
That's been true
of every civilization.


01:16:09.240 --> 01:16:10.639
And so, I think
the bigger question


01:16:10.760 --> 01:16:12.239
for this body
to consider is...


01:16:12.400 --> 01:16:15.995
who do you want calling
the shots in space?


01:16:16.720 --> 01:16:18.631
We have to know
what's out there, Senator.


01:16:19.480 --> 01:16:20.879
We have to touch the stars,


01:16:20.960 --> 01:16:23.269
if only to ensure
our own survival.


01:16:23.440 --> 01:16:25.271
And only a man can do that.


01:16:27.160 --> 01:16:28.559
We'll get
John Glenn home safely


01:16:28.640 --> 01:16:30.119
because we have to.


01:16:31.120 --> 01:16:33.315
Because he has to tell us
what he saw.


01:16:36.880 --> 01:16:38.871
The Redstone rocket
went straight up.


01:16:39.560 --> 01:16:41.312
But the capsule
didn't have enough speed


01:16:41.400 --> 01:16:42.920
to go into orbit,
so it came back down.


01:16:43.800 --> 01:16:47.110
Essentially a triangle.
Earth, sub-orbit, Earth.


01:16:47.280 --> 01:16:48.872
That math was known.


01:16:49.080 --> 01:16:51.548
That took Shepard,
took Grissom.


01:16:52.400 --> 01:16:53.913
That cannot take Glenn.


01:16:54.880 --> 01:16:56.472
Now, the Atlas rocket...


01:16:57.280 --> 01:16:59.077
that can push us into orbit.


01:16:59.240 --> 01:17:02.357
Goes up, delivers the capsule
into an elliptical orbit.


01:17:03.240 --> 01:17:04.958
Earth's gravity
keeps pulling at it


01:17:05.120 --> 01:17:07.759
but it's going so fast,
it keeps missing the Earth.


01:17:07.920 --> 01:17:09.672
That's how it stays in orbit.


01:17:09.840 --> 01:17:12.115
Now, getting it back down...


01:17:12.960 --> 01:17:14.552
that's the math
we don't know.


01:17:16.640 --> 01:17:17.640
Yes, Katherine.


01:17:17.800 --> 01:17:20.473
So the capsule will spin
around the Earth forever


01:17:20.640 --> 01:17:22.551
because there's nothing
to slow it down?


01:17:23.280 --> 01:17:24.280
That's right.


01:17:24.440 --> 01:17:27.398
Slowing it down at precisely
the right moment,


01:17:27.560 --> 01:17:29.152
at precisely
the right amount,


01:17:29.320 --> 01:17:30.320
that's the task.


01:17:33.480 --> 01:17:34.480
Yes, Katherine.


01:17:34.640 --> 01:17:38.076
So it needs to move
from an elliptical orbit...


01:17:39.600 --> 01:17:41.670
to a parabolic orbit.


01:17:41.880 --> 01:17:42.880
Yes.


01:17:43.720 --> 01:17:45.711
That's the "Go/No-go."


01:17:45.880 --> 01:17:47.836
Now at this point,
it's a pin head.


01:17:48.480 --> 01:17:49.993
We bring him in too soon...


01:17:50.160 --> 01:17:51.798
He burns up
on reentry.


01:17:51.960 --> 01:17:53.188
That's right.


01:17:53.360 --> 01:17:54.679
We bring him in
too late...


01:17:54.840 --> 01:17:56.432
he's pushed
out of Earth's gravity.


01:17:56.600 --> 01:17:59.910
And any changes to mass,
weight, speed, time...


01:18:00.080 --> 01:18:03.436
distance, friction,
or a puff of wind...


01:18:04.360 --> 01:18:06.396
would alter the "Go/No-go."


01:18:06.560 --> 01:18:09.518
And we start
our calculations over.


01:18:09.920 --> 01:18:10.920
Yes.


01:18:11.680 --> 01:18:16.435
So we need to be able to
choose this reentry point.


01:18:16.600 --> 01:18:19.592
Now this "Go/No-go,"
this has to be exact.


01:18:19.760 --> 01:18:20.795
Sam.


01:18:21.440 --> 01:18:23.556
These are the latest
Redstone numbers


01:18:23.640 --> 01:18:24.959
in current capsule data.


01:18:25.120 --> 01:18:26.678
Obviously, we know
the launch spot.


01:18:26.760 --> 01:18:27.760
Thanks.


01:18:27.840 --> 01:18:29.592
A Pentagon briefing
should illuminate


01:18:29.680 --> 01:18:31.398
where the Navy
needs the recovery zone.


01:18:31.600 --> 01:18:33.431
Then we work backwards
from there.


01:18:34.200 --> 01:18:37.715
And the rest is on us,
so let's get to work.


01:18:38.440 --> 01:18:39.440
Uh, Mr. Stafford.


01:18:39.760 --> 01:18:40.760
What, Katherine?


01:18:40.800 --> 01:18:42.153
If I could
attend the briefings,


01:18:42.240 --> 01:18:44.390
I'd be more useful
to the project.


01:18:44.560 --> 01:18:46.391
Pentagon briefings
are closed door.


01:18:46.560 --> 01:18:49.393
Yes, but if we don't
have the information


01:18:49.480 --> 01:18:50.799
of the changes...


01:18:50.960 --> 01:18:51.960
we can't keep up.


01:18:52.160 --> 01:18:53.456
I need those changes
as they occur.


01:18:53.480 --> 01:18:54.600
As you said,
it's a pin head.


01:18:54.760 --> 01:18:56.512
Katherine, that's the job.


01:18:56.680 --> 01:18:58.238
You asked for this assignment,


01:18:58.320 --> 01:19:00.390
so just calculate
with what you have.


01:19:00.560 --> 01:19:02.516
Or we'll find
someone who can.


01:19:09.240 --> 01:19:10.275
Okay.


01:19:23.040 --> 01:19:24.917
Attagirl.


01:19:25.520 --> 01:19:27.590
What the hell are you doing?
You can't be in here.


01:19:27.760 --> 01:19:28.795
Who are you?


01:19:28.960 --> 01:19:30.871
I'm with
the West Computing Group.


01:19:32.120 --> 01:19:33.348
Dorothy Vaughan, sir.


01:19:33.520 --> 01:19:35.238
This is a very delicate
piece of equipment.


01:19:35.400 --> 01:19:37.176
I'm sorry, sir.
I'm just trying to be helpful.


01:19:37.200 --> 01:19:39.430
Uh-huh.
Bill, we've got numbers.


01:19:41.880 --> 01:19:43.518
What kind of numbers?


01:19:47.640 --> 01:19:48.993
Well, I'll be.


01:19:50.080 --> 01:19:51.149
What's your name again?


01:19:52.080 --> 01:19:53.274
Dorothy Vaughan.


01:19:54.480 --> 01:19:55.913
Dorothy Vaughan.


01:20:03.200 --> 01:20:05.634
Sir, the parameters
for Friendship 7.


01:20:06.040 --> 01:20:07.393
Oh, this is all moot.


01:20:07.560 --> 01:20:09.391
We're altering the
window on the capsule.


01:20:09.560 --> 01:20:11.040
When did this happen?
Six minutes ago.


01:20:11.240 --> 01:20:12.639
The math is changing.
Start over.


01:20:12.800 --> 01:20:16.076
Sir, if I could attend the briefings,
I could stay current...


01:20:16.240 --> 01:20:17.719
Katherine, we have
been through this.


01:20:17.880 --> 01:20:18.880
It is not possible.


01:20:19.040 --> 01:20:20.616
There is no protocol
for women attending.


01:20:20.640 --> 01:20:21.789
There's no protocol
for a man


01:20:21.920 --> 01:20:23.160
circling the Earth either, sir.


01:20:23.200 --> 01:20:25.839
Okay. That is just
the way things are.


01:21:00.080 --> 01:21:01.593
Colonel Glenn's
launch coordinates,


01:21:01.680 --> 01:21:03.160
accounting for
the window change, sir.


01:21:03.280 --> 01:21:04.508
I have told you this.


01:21:04.600 --> 01:21:06.120
Computers don't
author reports. Fix it.


01:21:06.240 --> 01:21:08.356
Those are my calculations.
My name should be on it.


01:21:08.520 --> 01:21:10.431
That is not the way
this works.


01:21:10.600 --> 01:21:12.955
Paul. What's
happening here?


01:21:13.120 --> 01:21:15.270
Mr. Harrison, I would like
to attend today's briefing.


01:21:15.440 --> 01:21:16.509
And why is that?


01:21:16.720 --> 01:21:19.359
Sir, the data changes so fast.
The capsule changes.


01:21:19.560 --> 01:21:20.959
The weight and the landing zones


01:21:21.040 --> 01:21:22.553
are all changing, every day.


01:21:22.720 --> 01:21:24.950
I do my work, you attend
these briefings,


01:21:25.040 --> 01:21:26.040
I have to start over.


01:21:26.600 --> 01:21:28.511
Colonel Glenn launches
in a few weeks.


01:21:29.080 --> 01:21:30.600
We don't have the math
figured out yet.


01:21:31.600 --> 01:21:33.079
Why is it she can't attend?


01:21:33.280 --> 01:21:34.456
Because she doesn't
have clearance, Al.


01:21:34.480 --> 01:21:35.913
I cannot do
my work effectively


01:21:36.120 --> 01:21:37.360
if I do not have
all of the data


01:21:37.440 --> 01:21:39.635
and all of the information
as soon as it's available.


01:21:39.800 --> 01:21:41.456
I need to be in that room,
hearing what you hear.


01:21:41.480 --> 01:21:43.152
Pentagon briefings
are not for civilians.


01:21:43.240 --> 01:21:44.416
It requires
the highest clearance.


01:21:44.440 --> 01:21:45.456
I feel like
I'm the best person


01:21:45.480 --> 01:21:46.640
to present my calculations...


01:21:46.720 --> 01:21:48.736
You're not gonna let this go, are you?
No, I am not.


01:21:48.760 --> 01:21:50.716
And she is a woman.
There is no protocol


01:21:50.840 --> 01:21:51.936
for a woman
attending these meetings.


01:21:51.960 --> 01:21:53.279
Okay, I get that part, Paul.


01:21:56.320 --> 01:21:57.833
But within these walls,


01:21:58.840 --> 01:22:00.319
who makes the rules?


01:22:00.480 --> 01:22:01.993
You, sir. You are the boss.


01:22:02.160 --> 01:22:04.799
You just have
to act like one. Sir.


01:22:14.160 --> 01:22:15.309
You keep quiet.


01:22:16.200 --> 01:22:17.269
Thank you.


01:22:18.200 --> 01:22:19.269
Good morning.


01:22:20.200 --> 01:22:21.428
Gentlemen.


01:22:30.440 --> 01:22:31.440
This is Katherine Goble


01:22:31.520 --> 01:22:33.875
with our Trajectory
and Launch Window division.


01:22:34.840 --> 01:22:37.195
Her work is pertinent
to today's proceedings.


01:22:38.440 --> 01:22:39.440
Come on.


01:22:40.680 --> 01:22:41.874
Wanna get her a chair?


01:22:43.720 --> 01:22:44.856
We have a
confirmed launch window...


01:22:44.880 --> 01:22:46.200
Get her a chair, please.
Yes, sir.


01:22:46.280 --> 01:22:47.336
We have a
confirmed launch window


01:22:47.360 --> 01:22:48.475
for Friendship 7.


01:22:48.640 --> 01:22:49.800
Let's discuss a landing zone.


01:22:50.360 --> 01:22:52.669
The Navy needs
a singular landing zone.


01:22:52.840 --> 01:22:55.115
20 miles square is what we
can service for retrieval.


01:22:55.320 --> 01:22:56.320
Outside of that


01:22:56.480 --> 01:22:58.198
we risk the capsule's recovery.


01:22:58.360 --> 01:23:00.635
We'd like three possible
recovery areas.


01:23:00.800 --> 01:23:02.160
We can't cover
half the damn ocean.


01:23:02.840 --> 01:23:03.955
With all due respect,


01:23:04.080 --> 01:23:05.798
our capsule's being
altered daily.


01:23:06.720 --> 01:23:08.995
We're orbiting the Earth
at what speed now?


01:23:10.000 --> 01:23:11.000
Uh...


01:23:15.160 --> 01:23:17.754
17,544 miles per hour...


01:23:17.920 --> 01:23:19.751
at the time the rocket
delivers the capsule


01:23:19.880 --> 01:23:21.074
into low space orbit.


01:23:24.680 --> 01:23:26.360
Well, that's one hell
of a speeding ticket.


01:23:28.880 --> 01:23:29.880
Okay.


01:23:29.920 --> 01:23:31.353
So we have
the vehicle's speed,


01:23:31.440 --> 01:23:32.555
the launch window...


01:23:32.720 --> 01:23:34.358
and for argument's sake,
the landing zone


01:23:34.440 --> 01:23:35.714
is the Bahamas.


01:23:35.880 --> 01:23:37.598
Should be enough
to figure the "Go/No-go"?


01:23:37.760 --> 01:23:39.239
Yeah, in theory, sir.


01:23:39.400 --> 01:23:40.992
We need to be past
theory at this point.


01:23:41.200 --> 01:23:42.838
We'll be able to
calculate a "Go/No-go"


01:23:42.920 --> 01:23:44.433
with that information.


01:23:44.600 --> 01:23:46.511
When exactly
is that gonna happen?


01:23:56.960 --> 01:23:57.960
Katherine.


01:24:00.800 --> 01:24:02.472
Have a go at it.


01:24:27.600 --> 01:24:32.628
The "Go" point for re-entry
is 2,990 miles


01:24:34.000 --> 01:24:36.639
from where we want
Colonel Glenn to land.


01:24:36.840 --> 01:24:39.070
If we assume
that's the Bahamas...


01:24:40.000 --> 01:24:44.710
at 17,544 miles per hour...


01:24:45.240 --> 01:24:46.468
upon re-entry...


01:24:47.240 --> 01:24:48.992
370 feet...


01:24:49.600 --> 01:24:51.318
at a descent angle...


01:24:51.520 --> 01:24:54.830
of 46.56 degrees.


01:24:55.480 --> 01:24:56.480
Distance...


01:24:57.120 --> 01:25:00.635
velocity squared, sine...


01:25:00.800 --> 01:25:02.995
gravity squared.


01:25:04.960 --> 01:25:07.554
Sine, 32 feet.


01:25:08.440 --> 01:25:11.273
And the distance would be...


01:25:11.440 --> 01:25:17.197
20,530,372 feet...


01:25:18.680 --> 01:25:23.037
or 2,990 miles...


01:25:23.960 --> 01:25:28.875
or 46.33 degrees.


01:25:31.160 --> 01:25:33.628
Okay, so that puts
your landing zone


01:25:33.720 --> 01:25:37.349
at 5.0667 degrees North...


01:25:37.520 --> 01:25:40.512
77.3333 degrees West.


01:25:41.840 --> 01:25:43.512
Which is here.


01:25:46.480 --> 01:25:48.072
Right here.


01:25:48.240 --> 01:25:50.356
Give or take
20 square miles.


01:25:55.320 --> 01:25:56.514
I like her numbers.


01:25:58.000 --> 01:25:59.194
Thank you.


01:26:00.240 --> 01:26:01.832
That, of course,
is assuming


01:26:01.920 --> 01:26:04.559
the capsule hits
the re-entry point exactly.


01:26:05.160 --> 01:26:07.196
How do we ensure that?


01:26:07.920 --> 01:26:09.911
That's the math we don't
have yet, gentlemen.


01:26:10.400 --> 01:26:11.833
We're working on it.


01:26:14.080 --> 01:26:15.229
Katherine.


01:26:19.080 --> 01:26:21.376
What's it gonna take to make
this thing worth the price tag?


01:26:21.400 --> 01:26:23.755
We're just getting up
to speed here, Mr. Harrison.


01:26:23.880 --> 01:26:24.915
I need more manpower.


01:26:25.080 --> 01:26:26.877
I need programmers,
folks to feed the cards.


01:26:27.040 --> 01:26:28.519
Get them,
for crying out loud.


01:26:28.600 --> 01:26:29.600
Okay.


01:26:29.760 --> 01:26:31.716
You're the IBM man, right?
Yes, sir.


01:26:31.880 --> 01:26:33.711
Go pull them out of the sky
if you have to.


01:26:33.880 --> 01:26:35.576
Let's get ahead of this thing
or I'm not paying you.


01:26:35.600 --> 01:26:36.828
Understood.


01:26:40.120 --> 01:26:42.190
I got that one,
I got that one.


01:26:43.600 --> 01:26:45.352
I'm not paying any of you.


01:26:54.960 --> 01:26:55.960
Me?


01:26:56.120 --> 01:26:57.758
Temporarily, yes.


01:26:57.920 --> 01:27:00.036
We need the IBM
for Glenn's launch.


01:27:00.200 --> 01:27:02.160
The lead engineer says
you're good with the cards,


01:27:02.200 --> 01:27:03.519
programming and such.


01:27:04.080 --> 01:27:05.752
What about the girls here?


01:27:05.920 --> 01:27:08.388
Human computers can't
calculate an orbital flight


01:27:08.480 --> 01:27:09.879
in the time we have.


01:27:10.040 --> 01:27:11.473
They'll stay put for now.


01:27:11.640 --> 01:27:13.312
What about after now?


01:27:14.080 --> 01:27:16.548
After Glenn's launch,


01:27:16.640 --> 01:27:18.949
NASA's dissolving
the computing groups.


01:27:21.320 --> 01:27:23.231
I'm not accepting reassignment


01:27:23.320 --> 01:27:25.311
unless I bring my
ladies with me.


01:27:26.120 --> 01:27:27.235
Excuse me?


01:27:27.400 --> 01:27:29.914
We're gonna need a lot of
manpower to program that beast.


01:27:30.080 --> 01:27:31.308
I can't do it alone.


01:27:31.480 --> 01:27:34.631
My gals are ready.
They can do the work.


01:27:38.240 --> 01:27:39.309
Ladies.


01:27:41.760 --> 01:27:43.352
We've been reassigned.


01:27:44.760 --> 01:27:45.909
Leave your calculators.


01:27:46.000 --> 01:27:47.513
You won't need them
where we're going.


01:28:47.920 --> 01:28:49.751
Welcome, ladies.


01:28:59.920 --> 01:29:02.070
You know
what your job is, Paul?


01:29:03.520 --> 01:29:05.590
Find the genius
among those geniuses.


01:29:06.080 --> 01:29:07.433
To pull us all up.


01:29:08.600 --> 01:29:10.397
We all get
to the peak together


01:29:11.280 --> 01:29:12.998
or we don't
get there at all.


01:29:16.280 --> 01:29:18.430
Yes.
Well, good night, sir.


01:29:47.880 --> 01:29:50.030
I don't know what time
I'm coming home.


01:29:50.200 --> 01:29:51.872
Class ends at nine.


01:29:54.960 --> 01:29:56.473
You got something to say?


01:29:57.560 --> 01:29:59.437
I thought
you might need these.


01:30:02.920 --> 01:30:05.070
You can refill them with
any weight lead you want.


01:30:06.480 --> 01:30:09.313
You see, you just
hit the top like this.


01:30:10.160 --> 01:30:12.151
All right?
The lead comes out.


01:30:12.920 --> 01:30:14.638
You put the new lead in...


01:30:14.840 --> 01:30:17.673
and new pencil.


01:30:21.320 --> 01:30:23.595
You gonna make a fine engineer.


01:30:24.600 --> 01:30:25.600
You think so?


01:30:25.760 --> 01:30:26.829
Yes.


01:30:27.840 --> 01:30:30.752
And nobody dare stand
in the way...


01:30:30.920 --> 01:30:33.354
of Mary Jackson's dreams.


01:30:33.480 --> 01:30:34.913
Myself included.


01:30:40.360 --> 01:30:42.590
Don't make me mad again.


01:30:42.760 --> 01:30:43.988
Yes, ma'am.


01:30:47.680 --> 01:30:49.591
And don't
make me late, either.


01:30:49.760 --> 01:30:51.280
You gonna be late.
I'mma make you late.


01:30:51.480 --> 01:30:52.879
You won't be late.


01:30:59.440 --> 01:31:00.509
All right, gentlemen.


01:31:00.720 --> 01:31:01.720
Tonight, we discuss


01:31:01.800 --> 01:31:04.951
Planck-Einstein
wavelength relationships.


01:31:09.040 --> 01:31:10.155
Yes?


01:31:11.720 --> 01:31:12.789
I'm Mary Jackson.


01:31:12.960 --> 01:31:14.313
I'm enrolled.


01:31:16.640 --> 01:31:18.631
Well, the curriculum
is not designed


01:31:18.720 --> 01:31:19.869
for teaching a woman.


01:31:20.560 --> 01:31:22.551
I imagine it's the same
as teaching a man.


01:31:24.360 --> 01:31:26.316
I don't see a
colored section.


01:31:26.480 --> 01:31:28.232
Should I just
take any seat?


01:31:33.720 --> 01:31:34.835
Thank you.


01:31:39.880 --> 01:31:41.472
Okay. Uh...


01:31:41.920 --> 01:31:43.319
Planck-Einstein.


01:31:45.200 --> 01:31:46.200
The problem...


01:31:46.240 --> 01:31:49.437
is when the capsule moves
from an elliptical orbit


01:31:49.560 --> 01:31:51.073
to a parabolic orbit.


01:31:51.840 --> 01:31:54.229
There's no mathematical
formula for that.


01:31:54.440 --> 01:31:56.874
Because we can calculate
launch and landing...


01:31:57.040 --> 01:31:58.393
but without this conversion


01:31:58.560 --> 01:32:00.915
the capsule stays in orbit,
we can't bring it back home.


01:32:03.280 --> 01:32:05.120
Maybe we've been thinking
about this all wrong.


01:32:05.560 --> 01:32:06.560
How's that?


01:32:11.080 --> 01:32:12.718
Maybe it's not
new math at all.


01:32:14.760 --> 01:32:16.432
It could be old math.


01:32:17.520 --> 01:32:19.715
Something that looks
at the problem numerically,


01:32:19.800 --> 01:32:21.358
and not theoretically.


01:32:21.520 --> 01:32:23.272
Math is always dependable.


01:32:24.360 --> 01:32:25.634
For you it is.


01:32:32.120 --> 01:32:34.475
Euler's method.


01:32:34.640 --> 01:32:35.789
Yes.


01:32:35.960 --> 01:32:38.030
But that's ancient.
But it works.


01:32:38.640 --> 01:32:40.312
It works numerically.


01:33:16.520 --> 01:33:17.669
That's it.


01:33:20.120 --> 01:33:21.348
Let's type it up.


01:33:21.520 --> 01:33:22.520
Okay.


01:33:25.360 --> 01:33:26.429
Huh.


01:33:47.640 --> 01:33:48.834
Dorothy.


01:33:49.720 --> 01:33:51.039
Mrs. Mitchell.


01:33:57.360 --> 01:33:59.191
You're working late today.


01:34:00.160 --> 01:34:02.515
Seems to always
happen that way.


01:34:02.680 --> 01:34:04.671
Yes. Seems to.


01:34:12.520 --> 01:34:13.520
Thank you.


01:34:17.640 --> 01:34:20.393
I hear the IBM's
up to full capacity.


01:34:21.560 --> 01:34:23.198
You certainly
have a knack for it.


01:34:23.880 --> 01:34:26.678
My father taught me a thing
or two about mechanics.


01:34:27.560 --> 01:34:29.755
You know, I have a few
girls in the East Group


01:34:29.920 --> 01:34:32.229
that have an interest
in learning more about it.


01:34:32.400 --> 01:34:33.515
It is the future.


01:34:33.680 --> 01:34:35.989
I may bring them by,
if it's all the same to you.


01:34:36.880 --> 01:34:38.552
That's not my
decision, ma'am.


01:34:39.080 --> 01:34:40.752
That would be
the supervisor's.


01:34:41.720 --> 01:34:43.438
Yes, that's true.


01:34:45.680 --> 01:34:47.272
Good night, Mrs. Mitchell.


01:34:49.240 --> 01:34:50.719
You know, Dorothy.


01:34:52.440 --> 01:34:54.351
Despite what you may think...


01:34:55.560 --> 01:34:57.755
I have nothing against y'all.


01:34:57.960 --> 01:34:59.109
I know.


01:35:00.280 --> 01:35:02.430
I know you
probably believe that.


01:35:15.080 --> 01:35:16.280
Good night,
Katherine.


01:35:17.360 --> 01:35:18.475
Good night.


01:35:20.720 --> 01:35:22.438
Mama!
Hey, babies.


01:35:22.600 --> 01:35:24.431
Mama.
Hi.


01:35:25.120 --> 01:35:26.269
Hey.


01:35:26.760 --> 01:35:29.479
Oh, please don't tell me
I missed someone's birthday.


01:35:29.640 --> 01:35:30.640
No, you didn't.


01:35:30.800 --> 01:35:31.869
No, you didn't, Mama.


01:35:32.080 --> 01:35:33.638
Madame, your seat.


01:35:33.800 --> 01:35:35.552
Well, thank you.


01:35:36.640 --> 01:35:38.471
Wait, is it my birthday?


01:35:38.640 --> 01:35:39.789
No, Mama.


01:35:41.080 --> 01:35:42.479
You all look so pretty.


01:35:45.640 --> 01:35:47.835
Mama, what's going on?


01:35:50.960 --> 01:35:52.313
Good evening, Katherine.


01:35:58.320 --> 01:35:59.600
So you're not gonna
say anything?


01:35:59.760 --> 01:36:01.193
It's not my place.


01:36:03.000 --> 01:36:04.228
You, either?


01:36:06.960 --> 01:36:09.076
Okay, I'm not gonna
do this all night.


01:36:12.000 --> 01:36:13.479
Oh, mother of mercy.


01:36:14.200 --> 01:36:15.200
That's for me.


01:36:18.520 --> 01:36:20.636
Are you sad, Mama?
No.


01:36:20.800 --> 01:36:23.030
It's okay, Mama.
He's a good man.


01:36:23.200 --> 01:36:24.952
I know.


01:36:25.120 --> 01:36:26.416
I just can't help it.


01:36:26.440 --> 01:36:28.192
He hasn't even
asked you yet.


01:36:29.280 --> 01:36:31.157
I know, but he is.


01:36:31.320 --> 01:36:32.320
You are, right?


01:36:32.480 --> 01:36:33.708
If y'all will let me.


01:36:33.880 --> 01:36:35.996
Well, go on.
She's already crying.


01:36:36.160 --> 01:36:37.513
Oh, hush.


01:36:37.680 --> 01:36:38.720
This was my mother's ring.


01:36:39.520 --> 01:36:40.953
It's beautiful.


01:36:41.720 --> 01:36:43.160
She and my father
were married for...


01:36:43.320 --> 01:36:44.548
For 52 years.


01:36:46.480 --> 01:36:48.550
Figure it's about as lucky
a ring as you can get.


01:36:50.400 --> 01:36:52.470
The day I met you,
I called my mother


01:36:52.640 --> 01:36:55.712
and I said, "Mom, I met the
woman I want to marry."


01:36:55.880 --> 01:36:57.074
What'd she say?


01:36:57.240 --> 01:36:58.776
She said, "She must be
something, Jimmy.


01:36:58.800 --> 01:36:59.800
"Really something."


01:37:00.000 --> 01:37:01.069
Then what'd you say?


01:37:01.240 --> 01:37:02.468
I said, "No.


01:37:03.680 --> 01:37:05.557
"She's more than something.


01:37:05.720 --> 01:37:07.233
"She's everything."


01:37:08.080 --> 01:37:09.080
Now...


01:37:09.920 --> 01:37:11.478
I planned on this being
more romantic.


01:37:11.560 --> 01:37:12.896
I'll make that
up to you real soon.


01:37:12.920 --> 01:37:13.989
It's okay.


01:37:15.320 --> 01:37:17.072
But I know marrying you


01:37:18.000 --> 01:37:19.592
includes marrying
your girls as well.


01:37:21.680 --> 01:37:23.079
Amen to that.


01:37:23.760 --> 01:37:24.760
I do.


01:37:24.920 --> 01:37:26.751
I didn't ask you yet.


01:37:26.920 --> 01:37:28.035
Hurry up.


01:37:30.720 --> 01:37:32.392
Oh, my goodness.


01:37:33.240 --> 01:37:34.240
Oh!


01:37:35.600 --> 01:37:39.115
My babies. Look, girls,
isn't it beautiful?


01:37:40.080 --> 01:37:41.195
Look, Mama.


01:37:42.960 --> 01:37:45.000
The
Mercury-Atlas space vehicle...


01:37:45.120 --> 01:37:46.160
which will put John Glenn,


01:37:46.200 --> 01:37:47.800
the first American,
into Earth's orbit...


01:37:47.880 --> 01:37:51.031
has already undergone
five unmanned flight tests.


01:37:51.200 --> 01:37:52.200
NASA has confirmed


01:37:52.280 --> 01:37:55.033
that the IBM 790
data processing system...


01:37:55.200 --> 01:37:57.350
has been utilized to confirm
all of the mission 's...


01:37:57.520 --> 01:37:59.795
launch and recovery
system calculations.


01:37:59.960 --> 01:38:01.720
The modifications
and equipment installations


01:38:01.800 --> 01:38:03.074
are progressing on schedule.


01:38:03.240 --> 01:38:04.958
Yes, sir. Right in here.


01:38:06.640 --> 01:38:07.640
You wanted
to see me, sir?


01:38:07.800 --> 01:38:08.800
Have a seat.


01:38:09.920 --> 01:38:11.592
Is there something wrong,
Mr. Harrison?


01:38:11.760 --> 01:38:13.318
No, there's nothing wrong.


01:38:15.080 --> 01:38:16.877
In fact, our IBM
is churning out numbers


01:38:16.960 --> 01:38:19.110
in fractions of the time
any human can.


01:38:19.280 --> 01:38:20.952
Present company included.


01:38:21.640 --> 01:38:23.119
That's good then.


01:38:26.000 --> 01:38:27.149
Maybe.


01:38:28.160 --> 01:38:29.640
The thing is,
the Cape's now insisting


01:38:29.760 --> 01:38:32.035
on doing all the backups
down there, on site.


01:38:33.280 --> 01:38:34.280
I see.


01:38:34.320 --> 01:38:36.311
So we won't be
running backups here.


01:38:37.560 --> 01:38:40.313
And truth be told, we can't
keep up with that IBM anyhow.


01:38:43.680 --> 01:38:45.511
Long story short,
we no longer need


01:38:45.600 --> 01:38:47.158
a computer in this department.


01:38:48.760 --> 01:38:50.671
Progress
is a double-edged sword.


01:38:55.920 --> 01:38:58.309
Let's have you report back
to the West Group for now.


01:38:58.480 --> 01:39:00.277
We'll see if we can find
another assignment.


01:39:00.960 --> 01:39:02.313
Thank you, sir.


01:39:06.200 --> 01:39:07.200
Katherine, I'm...


01:39:09.360 --> 01:39:10.952
I'm sorry about this.


01:39:12.040 --> 01:39:14.190
It's out of my hands,
if you can believe that.


01:39:15.520 --> 01:39:16.794
I understand.


01:39:40.040 --> 01:39:41.040
Katherine.


01:39:43.200 --> 01:39:44.872
We heard you got engaged.


01:39:45.080 --> 01:39:46.080
Yes.


01:39:46.800 --> 01:39:47.994
That's thoughtful of you.


01:39:55.360 --> 01:39:56.839
They're lovely.


01:39:57.000 --> 01:39:58.831
Mr. Harrison's idea, really.


01:39:59.400 --> 01:40:02.358
Although I suspect his
wife did the picking out.


01:40:02.520 --> 01:40:05.353
Can't imagine he has an eye
for anything but numbers.


01:40:06.760 --> 01:40:08.239
Now you have
a great wedding.


01:40:09.840 --> 01:40:11.751
You did good work
around here, Katherine.


01:40:41.440 --> 01:40:43.096
Hello there.
Good to see you.


01:40:43.120 --> 01:40:44.720
Hi, Reverend. How you doing?
How are you?


01:40:45.960 --> 01:40:49.157
I don't think I felt a thing
the first time I did this.


01:40:49.320 --> 01:40:50.799
I was so nervous.


01:40:52.960 --> 01:40:54.016
You're just
as beautiful


01:40:54.040 --> 01:40:55.189
as you were back then.


01:40:55.400 --> 01:40:56.992
- You think so?
- Mmm.


01:40:57.640 --> 01:40:59.119
Just a little older.


01:40:59.480 --> 01:41:01.550
And a little fuller.


01:41:01.640 --> 01:41:02.959
But you look good.


01:41:07.320 --> 01:41:09.231
Well, here we go. Again.


01:41:09.720 --> 01:41:10.776
Project Mercury...


01:41:10.800 --> 01:41:12.153
named after the winged messenger


01:41:12.240 --> 01:41:14.674
of the gods in
Roman mythology. Today,


01:41:14.760 --> 01:41:17.320
three years, two months,
and three days afterwards...


01:41:17.880 --> 01:41:19.456
we're ready,
after 10 scheduled attempts...


01:41:19.480 --> 01:41:20.959
Attagirl.


01:41:21.120 --> 01:41:22.997
to try again to put
our first man into orbit...


01:41:23.160 --> 01:41:24.798
to put Colonel John Glenn
into orbit.


01:41:24.880 --> 01:41:27.440
Colonel John Glenn will be the
first American to orbit the Earth.


01:41:28.040 --> 01:41:29.040
As you can see, there are


01:41:29.160 --> 01:41:30.360
thousands of people here today


01:41:30.480 --> 01:41:32.311
to witness this historic event.


01:41:33.320 --> 01:41:34.833
Paul, something's off here.


01:41:35.000 --> 01:41:37.150
These landing coordinates
don't match yesterday's.


01:41:39.360 --> 01:41:40.839
The IBM just ran them.


01:41:41.800 --> 01:41:43.791
Well, then the IBM
was wrong yesterday.


01:41:44.480 --> 01:41:45.515
Right?


01:41:46.360 --> 01:41:47.839
Or the damn thing's wrong today.


01:41:51.720 --> 01:41:53.790
Paul, am I right?


01:41:54.640 --> 01:41:56.198
It appears so.


01:41:58.360 --> 01:42:00.999
The IBM's been spot-on
up to this point, John.


01:42:01.080 --> 01:42:03.196
But we'll run it again,
see what it comes up with.


01:42:03.360 --> 01:42:05.078
Look, I'm gonna be
honest with you, Al.


01:42:05.240 --> 01:42:07.196
When I fly,
I fly the machine.


01:42:07.360 --> 01:42:09.715
And right now it seems like
this machine is flying me.


01:42:09.880 --> 01:42:11.040
We're on the same page, John.


01:42:11.080 --> 01:42:12.354
Our guys are on it.


01:42:12.560 --> 01:42:14.312
Let's get the girl
to check the numbers.


01:42:15.080 --> 01:42:16.832
- The girl?
- Yes, sir.


01:42:18.760 --> 01:42:19.829
You mean Katherine?


01:42:20.000 --> 01:42:21.991
Yes, sir. The smart one.


01:42:22.160 --> 01:42:24.549
I mean, she says they're good,
I'm ready to go.


01:42:25.720 --> 01:42:26.880
All right. We'll get into it.


01:42:27.040 --> 01:42:28.268
Roger.


01:42:30.360 --> 01:42:31.360
Sam.


01:42:32.240 --> 01:42:33.719
Go find Katherine Goble.


01:42:33.880 --> 01:42:36.075
She needs to verify
Glenn's "Go/No-go,"


01:42:36.240 --> 01:42:37.440
or we're staying
on the ground.


01:42:37.560 --> 01:42:38.709
Yes, sir.


01:43:02.560 --> 01:43:04.118
I'm looking for Katherine Goble.


01:43:04.320 --> 01:43:05.469
It's Katherine Johnson now.


01:43:05.640 --> 01:43:07.280
They need you to verify
these coordinates.


01:43:08.320 --> 01:43:10.959
...and tourists have poured
into Cocoa Beach for weeks.


01:43:11.120 --> 01:43:13.156
Took this whole area
surrounding Cape Canaveral


01:43:13.280 --> 01:43:14.759
and Florida, really.


01:43:14.920 --> 01:43:17.150
And all around the country,
it's as if time...


01:43:17.320 --> 01:43:19.390
All right, give her
some space. Let her work.


01:43:19.560 --> 01:43:23.235
...as we await
Colonel John Glenn's launch.


01:43:23.400 --> 01:43:25.311
A truly historic day
for America.


01:43:25.480 --> 01:43:28.278
The country has waited
for several months,


01:43:28.440 --> 01:43:31.477
through many failed unmanned
Atlas rocket tests


01:43:31.680 --> 01:43:34.638
and 10 scheduled
manned attempts...


01:43:34.800 --> 01:43:36.870
which were canceled
for various mechanical


01:43:36.960 --> 01:43:38.791
or system complications.


01:43:38.880 --> 01:43:39.880
Even the weather...


01:43:39.960 --> 01:43:41.616
All the final tests and checks


01:43:41.640 --> 01:43:42.755
are being conducted.


01:43:42.920 --> 01:43:45.639
John Glenn is ready to board
the Friendship 7.


01:43:45.800 --> 01:43:48.030
But today we remain
truly optimistic,


01:43:48.120 --> 01:43:50.509
and it's a "Go" day
in all regards.


01:43:50.680 --> 01:43:52.272
The capsule is ready.


01:43:52.440 --> 01:43:54.158
The rocket itself is A-Okay,


01:43:54.320 --> 01:43:56.117
and the weather here
at Cape Canaveral...


01:43:56.280 --> 01:43:58.475
Anything from Langley?
Not yet.


01:43:58.640 --> 01:43:59.789
We are being told


01:43:59.960 --> 01:44:01.473
that Mission Control at NASA


01:44:01.640 --> 01:44:03.870
is conducting
a final check of the launch


01:44:04.000 --> 01:44:05.149
and recovery coordinates.


01:44:05.320 --> 01:44:07.834
Including the "Go/No-go"
calculations...


01:44:08.000 --> 01:44:09.000
so crucial


01:44:09.040 --> 01:44:10.800
to a safe and successful
launch and recovery.


01:44:10.960 --> 01:44:13.520
After years of
waiting, and months of delay,


01:44:13.720 --> 01:44:16.712
Colonel Glenn is finally ready
for that space flight...


01:44:16.880 --> 01:44:19.000
that we've been waiting for
with so much anticipation.


01:44:19.160 --> 01:44:20.976
These last-minute
checks could indicate


01:44:21.000 --> 01:44:22.877
a hesitancy on NASA's behalf.


01:44:23.040 --> 01:44:24.520
But let us say,
without reservation...


01:44:25.320 --> 01:44:27.197
that the safety
of Colonel John Glenn


01:44:27.360 --> 01:44:28.560
is paramount to the mission...


01:44:29.000 --> 01:44:30.513
and to the nation itself.


01:44:30.680 --> 01:44:31.829
No, sir.
We're still a go.


01:44:32.360 --> 01:44:33.554
Yes, sir.


01:44:37.320 --> 01:44:38.320
That's it.


01:44:43.040 --> 01:44:45.120
What the devil are you doing?
Are you taking a break?


01:44:49.320 --> 01:44:50.514
Yes!


01:44:51.200 --> 01:44:53.031
Say yeah


01:44:53.320 --> 01:44:54.355
Yeah


01:44:55.240 --> 01:44:56.719
Say, yeah!


01:44:57.560 --> 01:44:58.560
Yeah


01:45:00.840 --> 01:45:04.230
Don't you know,
we never ever


01:45:05.040 --> 01:45:07.235
Never know
what makes us major


01:45:08.480 --> 01:45:11.199
Your and my alchemy


01:45:11.280 --> 01:45:12.395
Yes, we can


01:45:12.520 --> 01:45:15.239
One and one equals three


01:45:15.400 --> 01:45:16.549
Yes, we can


01:45:16.680 --> 01:45:18.830
I feel it like lightning
in the forest


01:45:19.400 --> 01:45:20.400
Yes, we can


01:45:20.520 --> 01:45:23.239
Pick a number, pick a note
Harmonize the chords


01:45:23.400 --> 01:45:25.470
You're my woman
don't need another


01:45:25.560 --> 01:45:27.240
Get behind the other
We can lift each other


01:45:27.280 --> 01:45:29.589
It's all very well
that we may never...


01:45:29.760 --> 01:45:30.760
Whoo!


01:45:32.520 --> 01:45:33.919
Do you have the time?


01:45:35.280 --> 01:45:36.315
Yes, we can


01:45:36.400 --> 01:45:38.630
Like a solar system
they're surrounded by you


01:45:39.400 --> 01:45:40.400
Yes, we can


01:45:40.800 --> 01:45:43.075
Pick a number, pick a note
Harmonize the chords


01:45:43.240 --> 01:45:45.231
You're my woman
don't need another


01:45:45.400 --> 01:45:47.231
Get behind the other
we can lift each other


01:45:47.400 --> 01:45:49.436
...It's all very well
that we may never...


01:45:49.720 --> 01:45:50.720
Whoo!


01:45:51.240 --> 01:45:52.992
You're my woman
Don't need another


01:45:53.080 --> 01:45:55.435
Get behind the other
We can lift each other


01:45:57.040 --> 01:45:58.075
Whoo!


01:46:05.480 --> 01:46:06.480
Sir.


01:46:11.080 --> 01:46:12.115
Pad 14.


01:46:24.160 --> 01:46:25.160
Katherine.


01:46:30.440 --> 01:46:31.440
Come on.


01:46:35.080 --> 01:46:36.911
Sir, we've got
Pad 14 on the box.


01:46:37.480 --> 01:46:38.680
All right.
Let me in there.


01:46:42.120 --> 01:46:44.634
This is Langley, we have
the coordinates confirmed.


01:46:44.800 --> 01:46:46.320
Stand by, Langley.


01:46:48.760 --> 01:46:49.760
We can confirm...


01:46:49.960 --> 01:46:52.793
the "Go/No-go" point
for re-entry is...


01:46:52.960 --> 01:46:58.318
16.11984 degrees latitude...


01:46:58.480 --> 01:47:04.032
-165.2356 degrees longitude.


01:47:05.280 --> 01:47:06.998
The launch window is a go.


01:47:07.160 --> 01:47:09.151
The landing coordinates match.


01:47:09.360 --> 01:47:10.952
That is very good news, Al.


01:47:11.720 --> 01:47:14.280
It's a little hard to trust something
you can't look in the eyes.


01:47:14.360 --> 01:47:15.839
That's right, Colonel.


01:47:16.000 --> 01:47:17.718
Katherine did manage
to calculate


01:47:17.880 --> 01:47:19.996
a few decimal points further
than that hunk of metal.


01:47:20.840 --> 01:47:22.637
I will take every digit
you got.


01:47:22.720 --> 01:47:23.840
Be sure to thank her for me.


01:47:24.520 --> 01:47:26.192
Gentlemen,
let's launch this rocket.


01:47:26.360 --> 01:47:27.679
Good luck, Friendship 7.


01:47:27.840 --> 01:47:29.319
Godspeed, Langley.


01:47:32.640 --> 01:47:34.039
Here
at Cape Canaveral...


01:47:34.200 --> 01:47:35.758
the countdown has resumed...


01:47:35.920 --> 01:47:37.478
as Colonel John Glenn...


01:47:37.680 --> 01:47:39.238
is now aboard
the Friendship 7...


01:47:39.400 --> 01:47:41.755
high above the
Atlas rocket on Pad 14.


01:47:42.400 --> 01:47:44.516
A height of over 93 feet.


01:47:44.680 --> 01:47:45.760
So far, the countdown is...


01:47:45.840 --> 01:47:48.229
These are pictures
from Cape Canaveral, Florida.


01:47:48.440 --> 01:47:50.510
The gantry is now moving back.


01:47:50.680 --> 01:47:52.398
We are go for launch.


01:47:52.560 --> 01:47:55.154
The Mercury
spacecraft umbilical is out.


01:47:56.080 --> 01:47:58.036
T-minus 10 seconds,
and counting.


01:47:58.240 --> 01:47:59.496
Water systems, go.
Mercury capsule, go.


01:47:59.520 --> 01:48:00.576
Eight, seven...


01:48:00.600 --> 01:48:02.431
Six, five...
Ready engine. Start.


01:48:02.600 --> 01:48:03.696
four...


01:48:03.720 --> 01:48:05.870
three, two...
Good Lord, ride all the way.


01:48:06.080 --> 01:48:08.196
One, zero.


01:48:08.360 --> 01:48:09.554
Ignition.


01:48:10.560 --> 01:48:11.709
Lift off.


01:48:15.960 --> 01:48:18.076
The M-A 6 vehicle
has lifted off.


01:48:21.040 --> 01:48:22.560
It's getting
a little bumpy along here.


01:48:22.640 --> 01:48:24.790
Roger that.
You are in Max Q.


01:48:24.960 --> 01:48:26.439
Roger that, CAPCOM.


01:48:26.640 --> 01:48:28.471
All systems
are reported go.


01:48:28.640 --> 01:48:30.119
20 seconds to SECO.


01:48:30.280 --> 01:48:32.191
The trajectory
is still A-Okay.


01:48:32.400 --> 01:48:33.435
Go for SECO.


01:48:34.440 --> 01:48:37.079
Roger that, CAPCOM.
Go for SECO.


01:48:41.720 --> 01:48:44.154
Zero G and I feel fine.


01:48:44.600 --> 01:48:47.114
John Glenn reports
everything looks good.


01:48:47.920 --> 01:48:50.434
Oh, that view is tremendous!


01:48:52.000 --> 01:48:53.956
Roger that, Friendship 7.


01:48:58.960 --> 01:49:00.757
You are go for orbit.


01:49:00.920 --> 01:49:02.399
Thank you, CAPCOM.


01:49:07.600 --> 01:49:09.750
All right.
Ten and a half hours to go.


01:49:09.920 --> 01:49:11.353
If we make seven
round trips.


01:49:12.800 --> 01:49:14.119
I could use a coffee.


01:49:15.920 --> 01:49:17.956
Well, as we
approach the end of the day


01:49:18.080 --> 01:49:19.638
here on
the Eastern Seaboard...


01:49:19.800 --> 01:49:22.519
Colonel Glenn is just
beginning his third orbit...


01:49:22.680 --> 01:49:25.194
three-quarters of the way
across the African continent.


01:49:25.360 --> 01:49:26.952
We see he's currently
over Zanzibar...


01:49:27.160 --> 01:49:29.628
which is the next
tracking station coming up.


01:49:29.800 --> 01:49:31.950
Thankfully,
the F riendship 7 capsule is...


01:49:40.800 --> 01:49:42.313
New assignment
came down the pike.


01:49:45.840 --> 01:49:48.035
Always changing
around here.


01:49:48.200 --> 01:49:50.111
It's hard to keep up.


01:49:52.800 --> 01:49:54.176
Seems like they're going
to need a permanent team


01:49:54.200 --> 01:49:56.031
to feed that IBM.


01:49:56.200 --> 01:49:57.633
How big of a team?


01:49:57.800 --> 01:49:59.711
Thirty, to start.


01:50:02.480 --> 01:50:04.710
Thank you for the information,
Mrs. Mitchell.


01:50:06.360 --> 01:50:09.318
You're quite welcome,
Mrs. Vaughan.


01:50:23.640 --> 01:50:24.675
Hmm.


01:50:54.280 --> 01:50:55.713
We have a warning light.


01:50:56.400 --> 01:50:58.356
CAPCOM 7,
I have a warning light.


01:50:58.520 --> 01:50:59.669
Stand by, Seven.


01:51:00.400 --> 01:51:02.231
Sir, we have a warning light.


01:51:05.920 --> 01:51:07.273
What are we
looking at, Paul?


01:51:07.440 --> 01:51:08.475
Uh...


01:51:08.560 --> 01:51:10.596
The heat shield
may have come loose.


01:51:10.760 --> 01:51:12.830
Seven, will you confirm
the landing bag


01:51:12.920 --> 01:51:13.920
is in the off position?


01:51:14.960 --> 01:51:16.439
Affirmative, CAPCOM.


01:51:16.600 --> 01:51:17.919
Al, we need
to get him down now.


01:51:18.080 --> 01:51:19.798
He could burn up
on re-entry.


01:51:28.760 --> 01:51:30.591
Come on, babies.


01:51:30.760 --> 01:51:31.875
There is a man in space.


01:51:32.080 --> 01:51:33.576
And we don't want to miss it,
so we gotta hurry.


01:51:33.600 --> 01:51:35.272
Something's wrong
with Glenn!


01:51:35.440 --> 01:51:36.800
What's going on?
Come on, let's go.


